window.AQA_PAPERS = [{"id":"aqa26-sample-p1","year":"2028","paper":1,"series":"Sample assessment material (first exam 2028)","source":"aqa","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"section":"A","marks":6,"text":"Ben Francis has demonstrated several entrepreneurial characteristics in his career.\nAnalyse how one of these entrepreneurial characteristics has contributed to the success of Gymshark Ltd.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["Demands of the question","• To demonstrate knowledge and understanding of how an entrepreneurial characteristic contributes to","business success (AO1)","• To apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Gymshark Ltd (AO2)","• To analyse how this entrepreneurial characteristic contributes to the success of Gymshark Ltd (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 2 response will identify an entrepreneurial characteristic that can be supported by the context","given and will provide a clear link between the characteristic selected and a measure of success of","Gymshark Ltd, such as profit or sales.","Indicative content","• Entrepreneurial characteristics which can contribute to the success of a business are personal","features of an entrepreneur such as being a risk taker to seize opportunities, hardworking to cope","with the pressure and workload, passionate to get things done, innovative to create new products","and ways of doing things (AO1).","• Ben Francis is a risk taker; he took the risk of dropping out of university to start Gymshark Ltd (AO1,","AO2). This allowed him to dedicate his time and energy to building the brand, identifying a gap in the","market, and therefore ultimately contributing to the company’s success. Taking risks can be essential","for innovation and seizing opportunities (AO3).","• Ben Francis is passionate about Gymshark Ltd, as shown by his willingness to invest his savings,","learn to sew, and attend bodybuilding events to promote the brand (AO1, AO2). This passion helped","sustain the business in its early stages and therefore raised brand awareness, which was crucial to","its later success (AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"section":"A","marks":6,"text":"Ben Francis wants to make the Gymshark Ltd brand as strong as Nike’s.\nAnalyse one way in which having a brand as strong as Nike’s could affect the price elasticity of demand for Gymshark Ltd’s products.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of how a brand might affect the price elasticity of","demand (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Gymshark Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse how a strong brand might affect the price elasticity of demand (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 2 response is likely to show understanding of how a strong brand, such as Nike, affects","customers and demand. There will be a logical and accurate line of analysis from the strength of the","brand to its impact on the value of the price elasticity of demand; it will clearly link the sensitivity of","demand to changes in price.","• At Level 1 the analysis is unlikely to logically connect the strength of the brand to the impact on how","sensitive demand is to price, for example, it may just focus on the impact on the level of sales or the","popularity of the product.","Indicative content","• A strong brand is a product or service that is clearly distinguishable from competitors, usually through","a logo or slogan and is one where customers are likely to be more loyal which could affect the","sensitivity of demand to price (AO1).","• Building a strong brand such as Nike, which is worth billions and globally recognised, may mean that","Gymshark Ltd has more loyal customers (AO1, AO2). Loyal customers are more likely to be","prepared to pay higher prices and be less sensitive to price changes of a brand they trust, therefore","meaning the price elasticity of demand is likely to become more inelastic (i.e. have a value of less","than 1) (AO3).","• Gymshark Ltd operates in a competitive and fast-moving market. Even with a strong brand, its price","elasticity of demand may remain relatively price elastic due to the presence of other strong","competitors like Decathlon. This could, therefore, mean customers still respond to price changes","despite brand strength (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"section":"A","marks":9,"text":"Assess whether increasing spending on influencers would lead to higher profits for Gymshark Ltd.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of the advantages and disadvantages of using","influencers (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Gymshark Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse the case for and against Gymshark Ltd spending more on influencers (AO3)","• to evaluate whether spending more on influencers to promote Gymshark Ltd would increase profits","(AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• Level 3 responses must include a judgement on whether spending more on influencers would","increase profits. This judgement may be qualified (for example, “it depends on…”).","• To reach the top of Level 3 (9 marks), the judgement must directly address the impact on profits, not","just weigh up the general pros and cons of using influencers.","• Responses may argue that spending more on influencers would either increase or decrease profits,","provided the reasoning is logical and supported.","Indicative content","• An influencer is someone who can affect the purchasing decisions, opinions, or behaviours of others","because of their relationship with their audience—especially on social media platforms. This can","have a positive or negative effect on the sales of a business and its reputation and profits (AO1).","• Influencers can offer a high return on investment due to their reach and engagement levels (as","shown in Appendix C) (AO1, AO2). If the cost of influencer marketing is low relative to the revenue it","generates, this could increase profits (AO3).","• Gymshark Ltd can select influencers who align with its brand (such as sportspeople) which may","resonate with its target market (AO1, AO2). This targeted approach could boost sales, and if revenue","increases more than costs, profits will therefore rise (AO3).","• Ben Francis already has a large social media following. Instead of spending more on external","influencers, Gymshark Ltd could leverage Ben’s personal brand to promote products at a lower cost","(AO1, AO2). This could lead to higher profits by reducing marketing expenses (AO3).","• There are risks associated with influencer marketing. If an influencer behaves inappropriately, it","could damage Gymshark Ltd’s brand image (AO1, AO2). This reputational risk could reduce sales","and harm profits (AO3).","Evaluation","The use of influencers seems to fit well with the Gymshark brand, target audience and previous","approach to non -traditional forms of promotion. These have worked to date suggesting further spending","may make sense and could generate more profits (AO2, AO4). However, it would depend on the costs of","the influencers relative to the impact on revenue. It would also depend on the selection of the influencers","and how risks were controlled; if there were thorough systems to check any potential issues and strict","legal clauses regarding behaviour, this might reduce concerns over what could go wrong and help","ensure that spending more on marketing in this area generated a worthwhile profit (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"section":"A","marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Gymshark Ltd’s current ratio indicates that the business was in a strong financial position in 2024.\nCalculate the current ratio for Gymshark Ltd in 2024 to support your answer.\nGive your answer to two decimal places.","kind":"assess","quant":true,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of the current ratio as a measure of the financial","position of a business (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to calculate the current ratio for Gymshark Ltd in 2024","(AO2)","• to analyse the financial position of Gymshark Ltd in 2024 (AO3)","• to make a judgement on whether Gymshark Ltd was in a good financial position in 2024 (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• Typically, responses in Level 3 will feature a correct calculation with effective analysis stemming from","it. We would expect to see a comparison between Gymshark Ltd’s current ratio and the industry","average. Evaluation could consider the context of the business situation before making an","appropriate, well-supported recommendation. We might expect some consideration of the limitations","of using the current ratio as a means of measuring the financial health of the business or the","limitations of only looking at a relatively short period of time for the business.","• At Level 2 there may be some incorrect calculations (own figure rule) but relevant chain of reasoning","stemming from this. Alternatively, the calculations may be accurate but with less developed analysis","than that at Level 3. There may be some consideration of Gymshark Ltd’s current ratio in relation to","the industry average, but this is likely to be more limited than that at Level 3. Judgements are likely to","have some reference to these calculations.","• Responses that feature a correct calculation, but no analysis or evaluation would be placed at the","bottom of this level (4 marks).","• A response may argue that Gymshark Ltd was in a strong financial position or that it was not. A","response may qualify any overall judgement made (for example, “it depends on”).","Calculation (AO1 and AO2)","• Current assets = inventory + receivables + cash","£74 210","+ £13 497","+ £54 676","= £142 383","• Current liabilities = payables due within a year + other current liabilities","£191 478","+ £586","= £192 064","• Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities","• Current ratio = £142 383 ÷ £192 064 = 0.74","• If the correct numbers are selected but there is a mathematical error (for example, addition or","division error) or if the wrong units are given, for example, £0.74 then this is an inaccurate","calculation.","• If the wrong numbers are selected, this is not creditworthy as a calculation.","• A correct calculation demonstrates knowledge and understanding and application and would be","awarded 4 marks (at the bottom of Level 2).","• An answer that is based solely on the current ratio calculation in isolation can gain a maximum of","6 marks.","Indicative content","• The current ratio is a measure of liquidity and is calculated using the equation: (current assets ÷","current liabilities). If the business is liquid this could contribute to a strong financial position (AO1).","• A low current ratio can suggest potential liquidity problems. A current ratio of 0.74 may cause","concerns in terms of the ability of the business to pay its current liabilities. The ratio is lower than the","UK clothing industry average which again suggests liquidity may be an issue (AO1, AO2). As a","result, the business may need to sell assets, take out a loan, delay payables to over a year; these","solutions each bring consequences such as interest payments or unhappy suppliers (AO3).","Evaluation","• The current ratio is lower than the average for the UK clothing industry suggesting a weak financial","position (AO2, AO4).","• We do not have information on past years, competitors or the external environment to compare.","Ideally, we would have these comparisons with other businesses, and an understanding of the","strategy would help to place these numbers in context. The figures give an indication of financial","position, but we would want to understand the reasons behind these figures (AO4).","• The comparison with the UK clothing industry may not be valid as Gymshark focuses on sports","clothing not all clothing; also, the conditions and objectives of each business should be considered","individually rather than assume this ratio should be the same as the UK clothing industry average","(AO2, AO4).","• There are limitations of only using the current ratio to assess the financial position of a business","because it only measures liquidity; a business may be liquid but not profitable and not generating a","high return on capital employed (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"section":"A","marks":15,"text":"Ben Francis has ambitious plans for Gymshark Ltd.\nEvaluate two sources of external finance that Ben could use to fund these plans and recommend which one he should choose.","kind":"evaluate","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of appropriate external sources of finance (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Gymshark Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse two sources of raising external finance (AO3)","• to evaluate two sources of external finance Ben could choose to fund Gymshark Ltd’s ambitious","plans and recommend which one to choose (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• Do not credit internal finance.","• Appropriate sources of finance must be considered (overdraft for example is not appropriate to raise","the amounts of finance required).","• Top-level responses will be firmly rooted in the context of ambitious plans.","• Learners may judge that a particular external source of finance is most effective for Gymshark Ltd","provided that it is appropriate to the context.","• Allow any appropriate sources of external finance.","Indicative content","External sources of finance relevant to financing ambitious plans include loans, private equity, sale of","shares. Loans have interest payments but no loss of control. Share issue and private equity involve loss","of ownership but no interest (AO1).","Loans","• One advantage of using loans is that there is no loss of control, as lenders do not have voting rights","and therefore cannot influence the direction of the business (AO1). When shares were previously","sold, Ben retained 70% of them, which suggests maintaining control may be important for him (AO1,","AO2) - this might be because giving up control could lead to decisions, such as in marketing, that","conflict with his vision, as the founder, of what the brand represents (AO2, AO3). Choosing a bank","loan would allow Ben to maintain control and therefore is the source of finance he should choose","(AO3. AO4).","• The suitability of a loan also depends on the nature of the ambitious plans and the returns they are","expected to generate (AO1, AO2). If the returns are high, the cost of borrowing may be justified","because they can cover the costs (AO3). However, loans carry risk due to fixed interest payments,","which must be made regardless of business performance (AO4).","Share capital","• There was demand last time Gymshark Ltd sold shares so there may be again if it goes public or","issues more shares so this would help raise funds (AO1, AO2). If Gymshark’s ambitious plans are","convincing and compelling it may find it easy to raise the funds, especially given the apparent brand","strength, and therefore this, initially is a cheap way of raising funds which does not incur interest","payments (AO2, AO3).","• Issuing share capital involves a loss of control. There are no fixed repayments (AO1) but in a","successful year shareholders may require high dividends which may be greater than the interest","payments would be, and this therefore reduces the retained profits available, and this could mean","less investment available for Gymshark Ltd, for example, to open more stores and implement its","ambitious plans (AO2, AO3).","Private equity","• Using private equity could allow Gymshark Ltd to remain as a Ltd but Ben would need to sacrifice","some ownership. This therefore means others have votes on what happens at the business (AO1,","AO2). However, Ben could choose an investor or venture capital firm with experience in physical","stores and/or the Asian market which might prove helpful. This might provide insight into issues in","that market such as customer needs or competitors and therefore help Gymshark Ltd make better","decisions and make less mistakes leading to better long-term rewards (AO2, AO3).","• The potential clash of objectives is an issue. Typically, private equity focuses on short term returns","which, if Ben takes more of a long-term view, may clash with his objectives. For example, there may","be less focus on training and investment for long term projects (AO1, AO2). This clash may mean","that decisions are halted, and business opportunities are missed, and it may mean that it causes","tension and people want to leave resulting is less effective staffing (AO3).","Possible links to sophisticated concepts (AO3) include","• Linking the fixed costs of interest payments to break-even output and margin of safety.","• Linking the impact of borrowing to the impact on financial ratios such as gearing and return on capital","employed.","• Linking the involvement of more investors to the importance of such a group in terms of interest and","power in a stakeholder mapping or disagreements between current and new owners using Kotter’s","resistance to change model.","Credit other examples of use of sophisticated concepts.","Evaluation","A key issue is likely to be linked to ownership and whether Ben and the current owners want to retain","control. If so, do they have sufficient funds to invest in the business? If not, and they want to maintain","control they might need to raise finance from loans which will incur interest charges. Whether this is","attractive or not will depend on the interest charges involved; if these are high this will be less appealing","as a source of finance (AO4).","If the current owners are willing to bring in new investors, they can make the business a public limited","company or bring in a private equity investor. The Gymshark brand is doing well, so it is likely that","shares would sell well and for a high price. Ben can control the proportion of the business he chooses to","sell. However, it will depend on:","• the owners’ willingness to do this which will also depend on the price they will get","• the objectives of the potential investors and whether the current owners are happy if this is different","from their own objectives - how much of a clash will there be? (AO2, AO4)","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5}},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"section":"B","marks":6,"text":"Apple often sets a high price for its products, such as Vision Pro, compared to its competitors.\nAnalyse one way in which setting a high price might affect the revenue Apple earns from its products.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of pricing and revenue (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to Apple setting a high price for its products, such as","Vision Pro (AO2)","• to analyse the impact of setting a high price on Apple’s revenue (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 2 response will clearly link setting a high price to the impact on revenue in the context of this","product. A response may argue that revenue will increase, decrease or be unchanged.","Indicative content:","• Revenue measures the value of sales, i.e. price multiplied by quantity sold. Setting a high price leads","to a high revenue per unit; the overall effect on revenue is linked to the level of sales (AO1).","• By setting a high price, Apple will earn more revenue on any item sold. If sales are high, then with a","high price the revenue will also be high. Apple products such as Vision Pro, have a strong brand and","often have technological advantages over competitors. This means that whilst competitors may be","cheaper, they may not be perceived as good as Apple and so Apple sales may remain high. Given","the technology and the brand, demand may be price inelastic and therefore a high price increases","revenue. This is because demand is relatively insensitive to price (AO1, AO2, AO3).","• If the potential advantages of Apple are not perceived as important, or perhaps there are some","design issues as with Vision Pro, then the high price may affect sales as customers choose","alternatives such as Meta’s product. This means that demand could be price elastic and therefore","revenue may fall with a high price compared to a lower one (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"section":"B","marks":6,"text":"Apple’s share price changed between September 2021 and September 2023 (Appendix E).\nAnalyse one factor that might have caused this change in share price.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of factors influencing share prices (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Apple and Appendix E (AO2)","• to analyse one factor that might have caused this change in share price between September 2021","and September 2023 (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 2 response will clearly link the factor selected to the impact on the Apple’s share price. It will","show a good understanding of what determines a share price.","Indicative content:","• A share represents part ownership of a business. Share prices are determined by market forces of","supply and demand (AO1).","• Apple’s share price rose from $142.65 per share to $171.21 between September 2021 and","September 2023. Shareholders buy shares for a dividend and because of future expected increase","in price enabling them to be sold at a profit (AO1, AO2). The increase in price could be due to a rise","in the demand for the shares. This could be linked to shareholders’ views of future dividends or share","price changes. They may have expectations that the profits for the company will grow as some","commentators are optimistic of long-term sales, the business will be worth more and the share price","will rise enabling it to be sold for even more in the future (AO3).","• Investment in new product development is higher in 2023 than 2021 and investors may think this will","lead to successful products and higher profits in the future (AO1, AO2). This could increase demand","for Apple’s shares which could have increased the price because if demand is higher than supply it","can pull up the price because of the high demand (AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"section":"B","marks":9,"text":"Assess whether an increase in spending on market research could be profitable for Apple.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of market research and how this might affect profit","(AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Apple (AO2)","• to analyse spending on market research (AO3)","• to evaluate whether an increase in spending on market research is likely to be profitable for Apple","(AO4).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 3 response will make a well-supported judgement on when increased spending by Apple is likely","to be profitable. This judgement may be qualified. For the very top of the level (9 marks), there must be a","clear sense of there being an increase in its spending.","Indicative content","• Market research provides information which can be used to identify market opportunities, assess","marketing plans and review their effectiveness. Market research involves the gathering and","analysing of data relevant to the marketing process. This can lead to better decisions and therefore","more profit but there are costs involved (AO1).","• Vision Pro has proven that Apple will not always know what consumers want. Increased spending on","market research could have prevented its launch (AO1, AO2). This could end up being damaging to","the brand and costing a lot of money in research and development and therefore reducing profits.","Having the right information may enable the right marketing mix to be developed and therefore","prevent losses being made (AO3).","• Apple has launched many successful products and may already have knowledge of what customers","want (AO1, AO2). It has a database with a large amount of customer information, making any new","market research potentially a waste of time as it may already have access to information to help","make decisions around products etc. This means that increased spending on market research may","be a waste of money and reduce profits from what they could have been (AO2, AO3).","• Apple operates in a very dynamic market. Market research takes time and therefore could slow down","product launches and result in Apple being less successful (AO1, AO2). Apple may feel its products","are anticipating what customers want but customers themselves may not know this yet, so the value","of research may be limited. Apple is very innovative, and customers may indeed not know they want","its products until they see them. This might suggest that market research would simply be a cost and","may not help profits (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Evaluation","• Apple is experienced in the market and has so much data, there may not be a need to conduct any","further market research. Market research could further add to the cost and reduce profits (AO2,","AO4).","• However, current product launches show the need for Apple to find out the needs of its customers.","Given the high spend on research and development, it is a costly mistake to make. Market research","may identify profitable opportunities, lead to better, more effective marketing mix decisions and","generate revenues which more than cover the costs of the research itself (AO2, AO4).","• It would depend on what the research was and how it was conducted. For example, if an","unrepresentative sample of people participated this might be of limited value (AO4).","• On the other hand, a ‘significant’ increase in spending might suggest that the research will be","extensive and could help lead to better marketing decisions and less waste. Done well, it might","improve marketing outcomes and therefore generate a high return on investment and more profits for","the business (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"section":"B","marks":9,"text":"Assess whether a large investment in new product development is likely to increase Apple’s market share.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of new product development and its impact on market","share (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Apple (AO2)","• to analyse how investment in new product development might affect market share (AO3)","• to evaluate whether a high level of investment in new product development is essential to a large","market share for Apple (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 3 response will address whether a high level of investment will increase market share. For the","top mark there must be a sense of ‘market share’ rather than just sales and whether this is ‘essential’ or","not.","Indicative content:","• New product development involves investing in developing products. Market share measures the","sales of a business relative to the total sales in the market. New product development might meet","customer needs better and lead to more sales and market share (AO1).","• New product development is crucial to a business such as Apple which operates in such a dynamic","market. Without new product development, there would be no new products to extend the sales and","life of the business in the long run because product life cycles do have a decline phase. Products","such as the iPhone represent one of Apple’s top-selling products, but the product life cycle suggests","the rate of growth of sales is slowing (AO1, AO2). Without new products, Apple may eventually see a","fall in long-term sales and its share of the market. This could mean sales and market share fall","(AO3).","• New products, such as new VR headsets will be launched by competitors such as Meta. If Apple","does not invest in new product development, it may find itself falling behind competitors (AO1, AO2).","This means it may miss out on emerging markets and or experience a declining market share in","current markets as competitors develop improved versions of their products (AO3).","• Apple has a large product portfolio, and some products have had a long maturity stage, such as the","iPhone, which seems to expect increasing sales, as shown in Appendix F (AO1, AO2). In this case","the amount spent on new product development might not to be as high as it could be for a business","that has products with shorter life cycles. Launching new products may reduce the sales of the","existing ones which could have gone on for longer and whose life cycle could have been extended","and therefore this might not increase market share (AO3).","• New product development is very expensive, as shown in Appendix D, (AO1, AO2) and for Apple it","may be better to try and increase market share by extending the life of its existing products through","advertising, price reductions etc at least in the short term. This is because it may be cheaper and","generate higher short-term returns (AO3).","Evaluation","• If competitors are looking to launch a lot of new products, it would be advantageous for Apple to","invest in new product development to remain competitive (AO4).","• New product development may not be essential to market share in the short term as Apple already","has a large product portfolio of well-established products but at some point, improved products or","new products will be required (AO2, AO4).","• It may be a question of how much investment in new product development Apple should undertake","and how well this investment was used. Developing new products in any market, but especially in","technology, is almost certainly essential in the long term, but it may be a question of the extent of","investment required. The question refers to high levels of investment; this may indeed be necessary","given the nature of the industry Apple is in, however, what also matters is how the money is used.","Much investment in new product development is wasted as the products do not make it to launch or","fail so the key is to use any investment well, not just spend more (AO2, AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"section":"B","marks":15,"text":"Appendix G shows market and financial data for launching the Vision Pro in two regions.\nEvaluate whether Apple should launch the Vision Pro in Region A or Region B.\nCalculate the return on investment in Region B in 2025 to support your answer.","kind":"evaluate","quant":true,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of relevant factors to choose which region to launch in,","including return on investment (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding of relevant factors to the choice of region (AO2)","• to analyse factors influencing the choice of region to launch the product in (AO3)","• to evaluate which region to choose based on the return on investment and other factors (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A response may argue that Apple should choose Region A or Region B. An overall judgement may","be qualified.","• A correct calculation of the return investment is worth 4 marks.","Calculation for Region B (AO1 and AO2)","Operating profit = 22% of $1500 million = $330 million","Return on initial investment for 2025 = ($330 million ÷ $3000 million) x 100 = 11%","Indicative content","• The return on investment measures profit as a percentage of the investment required and, other","factors being equal, the higher the return the more attractive an investment would be (AO1).","• The operating profit margin measures profit before interest and tax as a percentage of sales. The","higher this is the greater the profit per sale and potentially the more attractive a market will be -","although the size of the market should be considered to calculate the overall return (AO1).","• The income elasticity of demand measures the changes in quantity demanded in relation to income","changes; this may affect how sales grow in the future and therefore the future market size and how","attractive an investment is (AO1).","• The initial investment required to enter Region A is significantly less than Region B ($1.2 billion v $3","billion) (AO1 and AO2). A lower investment is a lower initial financial risk. This may be appealing if","funds are limited although potentially slightly less of an issue for a business the scale of Apple (AO2,","AO3).","• Although the market size of Region A is much smaller than Region B, the operating profit margin","(profit per sale) is higher and the overall return in Region A is higher (14% v 11%) (AO1, AO2). This","is because less is invested and so the relative return is higher [OFR for this analysis] and therefore","may be more attractive (AO2, AO3).","• Region B has a bigger market size and so even with a slightly lower operating profit margin, the total","operating profit in Region B is much greater ($330 million v $168 million) [OFR applies] (AO1, AO2).","However, the rate of return is lower given the higher initial investment which may make this Region","less attractive (AO2, AO3, AO4).","• The income elasticity of demand is higher in Region B (because it is elastic not inelastic) which","means that demand is more sensitive to income changes (AO1, AO2); given that average income","growth is higher in Region B this means demand should grow faster in this region which might make","it a more attractive long-term investment (other factors unchanged) (AO2, AO3).","Reference to sophisticated concepts may include:","• Reference to concept of risk and amount of risk Apple want to take with the initial investment.","• Reference to other methods of assessing an investment such as payback and net present value.","• Reference to Environmental, Social and Governance measures; Apple may not assess a region just","on financial data.","Evaluation","• Region A is cheaper to enter and operating profit as a percentage of the initial investment is higher. If","Apple was concerned about risking funds, Region A may be perceived as lower risk. Overall, Region","A has a higher return on investment despite smaller sales due to higher operating profit margins and","smaller initial investment. This may make this a more attractive region to launch at least in the short","term (AO2, AO4).","• Region B has a higher absolute operating profit and seems to offer better long-term opportunities.","Apple is likely to be entering a market for the long term and so the bigger Region B with more","potential given the income growth and income elasticity of demand is likely to be more appealing","(AO2, AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5}}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Gymshark Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Ben Francis was 19 when he asked his parents if he could use their garage to start making gym clothes he had designed. Whilst studying business at university, Ben teamed up with a school friend, and the gymwear business Gymshark Ltd was born."},{"t":"p","x":"Ben used his £5-an-hour wage at Pizza Hut and £1000 savings to invest in equipment like a sewing machine. Ben taught himself to sew and went to bodybuilding events to raise brand awareness. In 2012, Ben dropped out of university to focus on the business full-time."},{"t":"p","x":"The Gymshark Ltd brand has become a hit with younger gym goers and is doing well in a highly competitive market. Its competitors include Nike, Adidas and Puma. Instead of using traditional advertising or selling in high street shops, Gymshark Ltd has grown by having athletes and social media ‘influencers’ wear its clothes on platforms like Instagram, TikTok, and Facebook. Ben is also well known and still shares his own fitness videos online to promote the brand. The company now has 18 million followers on social media."},{"t":"p","x":"Gymshark Ltd is a private limited company. In 2021, it raised £200 million by selling shares to a company called General Atlantic. This made Gymshark Ltd one of relatively few British businesses valued at over $1 billion. Ben still owns a 70% stake in the company and has a clear vision of how he wants the business to develop. General Atlantic owns just over 20%. Managers at Gymshark Ltd own 10%. The funds were raised with the aim of expanding the business in North America, where Gymshark Ltd already makes a large part of its sales, and to grow the business rapidly in Asia. Ben has ambitious plans and wants to make the Gymshark Ltd brand the UK equivalent of Nike."},{"t":"p","x":"The Nike brand was worth $31 billion in 2023 globally; market research suggests that 43% of people who buy Nike sportswear would do so again."},{"t":"p","x":"In October 2022, Gymshark Ltd made a significant investment and opened its first physical store on Regent Street in London."},{"t":"p","x":"Source: information sourced from Gymshark, 2024"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Extracts from Gymshark Group Ltd’s statement of financial position as at"},{"t":"tbl","x":["July 2024","£000","Non-current assets 159 077","Inventory 74 210","Receivables 13 497","Cash 54 676","Payables due within a year 191 478","Other current liabilities 586","Non-current liabilities 102 920"]},{"t":"p","x":"Source: Companies House"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B: UK clothing industry fictional financial information"},{"t":"p","x":"Industry average current ratio = 1.4 Source: Analyst"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix C: Social networks by number of monthly active users globally, 2024"},{"t":"p","x":"Billions of users Age range of highest proportion of users"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Facebook 3.0 25–34","Instagram 2.0 25–34","TikTok 1.6 18–24","Source: Statista, 2024"]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Apple’s Vision Pro"},{"t":"p","x":"In September 2023, Apple launched the Vision Pro in North America. Vision Pro is a virtual reality (VR) headset. Unlike rival headsets which require hand-held controllers, Vision Pro is controlled by hand movements, voice commands and eye tracking. Analysts estimated over $8 billion had been spent developing Vision Pro over five years."},{"t":"p","x":"The Vision Pro may turn out to be one of Apple’s lowest-selling products. Apple reportedly hoped to sell 3 million units in the first year. Some experts expected sales would be below 200,000 units in this period – lower than any of Apple’s other major product launches."},{"t":"p","x":"Others were more optimistic about Vision Pro’s long-term sales. Apple set a high price of $3499. This price was three times more expensive than Meta’s competing headset, the Quest. Other issues with the product included a battery that lasted only two-hours and a headset that some users said was awkward to wear."},{"t":"p","x":"Apple was keen to release the Vision Pro because of strong competition, especially from Meta. Meta had been selling the Quest at a loss to increase its market share."},{"t":"p","x":"Steve Jobs, Apple’s founder, once said that customers do not know what they want until Apple makes it."},{"t":"p","x":"Over 2 billion Apple devices are in use around the world. Apple also has:"},{"t":"li","x":["a huge database of information about current users of its products.","a wide product portfolio including iPads, Mac computers, iPods, Apple TV, and the Apple watch.","a trusted brand. In a 2021 survey, three times as many people said they would buy a headset from Apple compared to its closest competitor."]},{"t":"p","x":"In 2023, Apple increased its dividend payments to about 15% of its yearly profits."},{"t":"p","x":"Source: Apple, 2024"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix D: Selected financial data for Apple in 2021 and 2023"},{"t":"p","x":"2021 $ millions 2023 $ millions Sales 365 817 394 328 Operating profit 108 949 125 344 Investment in new product development 21 914 29 915 Source: Apple, 2024"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix E: Apple share price"},{"t":"p","x":"30 September 2021 29 September 2023 $142.65 $171.21 Source: Apple"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix F: Apple sales by product, 2003 - 2023"},{"t":"p","x":"Source: Apple"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix G: Fictional market and financial data for launching Vision Pro in Region A and Region B"},{"t":"p","x":"Region A Region B"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Initial investment into the market ($ million) 1 200 3 000","Market size 2025 ($ million) 7 000 15 000","Market share (%) 10","Sales ($ million) 1500","Return on investment into the market in 2025 (%) 14 ?"]},{"t":"p","x":"Income elasticity of demand Inelastic Elastic Forecast average annual income growth (2025 - 2030)(%) 2 4 Operating profit margin (%) 22 Permission to reproduce all copyright material has been applied for. In some cases, efforts to contact copyright-holders may have been unsuccessful and AQA will be happy to rectify any omissions of acknowledgements. If you have any queries please contact the Copyright Team."},{"t":"p","x":"Where appropriate, real company data will be provided. However, in some instances, data may be fabricated solely for illustrative purposes (see Appendix G)."},{"t":"p","x":"Any such fabricated data is not affiliated with, nor does it represent, any actual business or organisation."}]},"caseNames":["Gymshark Ltd","Apple’s Vision Pro"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-1","title":"Sample set 1 · Paper 1"},{"id":"aqa26-sample-p2","year":"2028","paper":2,"series":"Sample assessment material (first exam 2028)","source":"aqa","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"section":"A","marks":6,"text":"Analyse one way in which teamwork might improve SHEIN’s financial performance.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of how teamwork might improve financial","performance (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of SHEIN (AO2)","• to analyse one way in which teamwork might improve SHEIN’s financial performance (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","For Level 2, there must be a clear, logical chain of argument from teamwork to the financial performance","of SHEIN. This may focus on any aspect of financial performance, for example, profit or liquidity.","Indicative content","• A team is a group of employees that work together to perform specific tasks working towards a","shared goal; this collaboration can help reduce costs and improve revenues thereby improving","financial performance (AO1).","• With nearly 10 000 employees globally, effective teamwork can be critical for SHEIN in ensuring","coordination between employees in different functions and countries (AO1, AO2). The success of","SHEIN’s lean production model is likely to depend on how effectively different teams communicate","and can contribute to continuously improving productivity. This can, in turn, lead to improvements in","profit margins and other measurements of financial performance through greater operating","efficiencies (AO1, AO2, AO3).","• Effective teamwork may be appealing to workers, helping SHEIN to attract and retain the best staff,","reducing costs in areas such as training and recruitment. Lower costs are important to SHEIN’s","model, which is evident as they operate a lean production system and aim to reduce waste","wherever possible (AO1, AO2). Maslow’s theory suggests that meeting social needs is important in","motivating staff as this helps employees feel connected to one another (AO1). This means","employees are more likely to be engaged and committed to the company’s objectives. Teams that","are structured to afford scope for input and recognition also activate even higher levels of Maslow’s","hierarchy, improving employee satisfaction and so meaning that employee turnover is reduced and","the associated cost savings can improve profit levels. Greater motivation can help staff increase","their effectiveness and high-performing staff may also differentiate the organisation, helping it to","increase sales revenue, reduce unit costs and improve profit margins (AO3).","• Herzberg suggests that job enrichment can be a motivator and individuals working in teams often","have increased task variety and scope for autonomy, improving job satisfaction and encouraging","input from team members at all levels (AO1). This can lead to greater creativity and better problem","solving due to the wider pool of ideas. This may allow SHEIN to achieve higher quality and so","improve its brand appeal, leading to increased market share and facilitating its achievement of the","aggressive revenue growth targets it has set itself and leading to better financial performance (AO2,","AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"section":"A","marks":6,"text":"Analyse one way in which improving the environmental sustainability of its operations might affect SHEIN’s management of its supply chain.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of how environmental sustainability might affect","SHEIN’s supply chain management (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of SHEIN (AO2)","• to analyse one way in which improving the environmental sustainability of its operations might affect","SHEIN’s management of its supply chain (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 2 response must show a clear link between environmental sustainability and how this affects","SHEIN’s management of its supply chain.","Indicative content","• Environmental sustainability in business refers to operating without negatively impacting the","environment, for example, less wastage. How environmentally sustainable a price will be affected by","the way in which the supply chain is managed (AO1).","• Supply chain management refers to the coordination of all the activities, organisations, and","resources involved in moving a product from raw materials to the final customer. This will affect the","environmental sustainability of a process (AO1).","• Reducing wastage within the supply chain is part of environmental sustainability (AO1). To achieve","this, SHEIN will work with its current supplier network to reduce any waste in the supply chain, have","better coordinated activities and reduce the wastage of materials at SHEIN itself. Efforts to improve","environmental sustainability will therefore involve greater coordination of activities with suppliers to","ensure the right quantities are ordered at the right time and used efficiently (AO2, AO3).","• Greater coordination can also reduce transportation of materials reducing the environmental impact","of pollution (AO1). SHEIN’s supply chain management can coordinate deliveries more efficiently to","its warehouse and therefore improve the environmental sustainability of its operations by reducing","the negative impact on the environment (AO2, AO3)","• SHEIN’s publicised intention to tackle wastage and circularity at every stage means it will want to","work with suppliers on the materials used and how to recycle materials; for example, there may be a","returns policy that allows customers to return old clothes for a discount on new purchases and these","clothes could be recycled and the materials re-used by suppliers (AO1, AO2). To improve","environmental sustainability, SHEIN may therefore want to select and work with suppliers willing to","partner in this more circular approach (AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"section":"A","marks":9,"text":"Assess the impact of SHEIN using lean production on its market share.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of lean production and market share (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of SHEIN (AO2)","• to analyse the impact of lean production on the market share (AO3)","• to evaluate whether lean production will impact SHEIN’s market share (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 3 response must weigh up arguments relating to the use of lean production and its impact","on market share. For the top mark, there must be a clear sense of the impact on market share","rather than just sales.","• A response may judge that the impact is high or low, positive or negative.","Indicative content","• Lean production involves a range of different techniques that all aim to reduce waste, for example, of","time, of materials, of labour. By being more efficient, this may help a business offer better value for","money and help increase the business’ sales as a proportion of total sales, i.e. increase its market","share (AO1).","• SHEIN competes in a low-price market which is very price sensitive. Its ability to keep costs low as a","result of lean production enables low prices (AO1, AO2). This helps the business to compete against","other businesses and gain sales at their expense increasing its market share (AO3).","• One lean production technique is Kaizen; this aims to drive small but continuous improvements in","processes. This should cut costs by reducing waste; if less time is wasted, less labour is employed,","fewer resources are used and so fewer items have to be re-worked (AO1). This then means SHEIN","can price its products very competitively relative to rivals, and this can help it gain sales and market","share (AO3).","• Lean production involves low levels of inventory and a just in time approach; this avoids the risks of","being left with unsold items. This means the business should have lower costs in terms of holding","inventory, lower costs in terms of security or theft of items in storage and lower costs incurred with","having unsold items (AO1). This helps efficiency and price competitiveness which is important to","SHEIN’s low price model to protect profit margins (AO2). With lower unit costs, the business can","offer lower prices and still generate an appropriate profit margin and gain market share (AO3).","• Lean production could support faster response times as it may allow SHEIN to streamline processes","and cut stages from the production process including interactions with firms further up the supply","chain. This means SHEIN may be able to respond more quickly to changing demand patterns in its","fast-moving market, for example, by imitating popular designs created by rivals or improving the","computer modelling programmes that predict demand (AO1, AO2). This agility may allow it to bring","new styles to market more quickly, which could be vital in this market and help SHEIN gain a bigger","proportion of sales in the market (AO3).","• Lean production also brings problems. By holding only a very low level of inventory, SHEIN may not","be able to respond immediately to a surge in demand, for example, due to changes in fashion (AO1,","AO2). It will be very reliant on suppliers and employees because there are no ‘spare items’. This","means there may be times when it has run out of items and customers may switch elsewhere","reducing SHEIN’s competitiveness and market share (AO3).","Evaluation","• It will depend on whether SHEIN can respond quickly enough to demand (and that depends on its","links with suppliers) to remain competitive. SHEIN seems to work well with and invest in its suppliers","which may make the value of lean production greater. This may reduce the risk of having problems","with suppliers and not having items to sell (AO2, AO4).","• Lean production can create problems if employees are not ‘on board’ with this approach and do not","cooperate or withdraw their labour, because again there are no items to sell. However, SHEIN seems","to value employees (or at least says it does) which may reduce this risk and again increase the value","of lean to the business (AO2, AO4).","• SHEIN already uses computer modelling to try to match supply to demand and, if it can harness the","latest digital technologies to bring together aspects of digital marketing through its website, with its","lean production model, it may be able to gain competitive advantage. However, other rivals are likely","to be investing heavily in similar process improvements and so a relentless, ongoing focus on","continuous improvement is likely to be required to maintain any advantage gained and increase","market share (AO2, AO4).","• The value of these benefits depends on how well lean production is implemented relative to other","producers; it only improves market share if done better than others. SHEIN’s rivals will also be","aiming to be as lean as possible and so the benefits to SHEIN always need to be considered relative","to other firms (AO4).","• Lean production can lead to greater market share if implemented more effectively than rivals;","however, there are potential problems which can reduce the appeal of the offering and lead to","customers switching elsewhere. Regardless of whether lean production can give SHEIN a","competitive advantage, it may well be important to continue using it, as ceasing to do so would likely","worsen its competitive position (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"section":"A","marks":9,"text":"Assess how SHEIN’s approach to quality might affect its profitability.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of quality and its impact on profitability (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of SHEIN (AO2)","• to analyse the impact of SHEIN’s approach to quality on its profitability (AO3)","• to evaluate whether SHEIN’s approach to quality might affect its profitability (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• To achieve Level 3, the response must clearly focus on profitability (for example, return on capital","employed or profit margins) rather than profits.","• Arguments suggesting profitability may be higher, lower or unchanged can all be accepted if","supported.","Indicative content","• Quality refers to the extent to which items are fit for purpose. Better quality can lead to fewer","mistakes thereby reducing costs and may justify a higher price and can lead to more sales and","higher profitability. Profitability is a measure of profit as a return in relation to, say revenue or capital","employed (AO1).","• At SHEIN, quality control checks at the warehouse can mean that faulty items are not stored and","distributed (AO1, AO2). This can reduce costs of returns and distribution which should improve","operating profit margin. This can also improve customer satisfaction which might increase revenues","and profitability if it leads to more repeat business (AO3).","• When employees are encouraged to see colleagues as internal customers as part of a quality","approach, it may ensure that they take ownership of their role in delivering quality in their aspect of","the production process (AO1, AO2). This level of ownership could satisfy employee desires for","responsibility, achievement and recognition – all motivators according to Herzberg’s two-factor theory","– boosting motivation and productivity. Therefore, unit costs may fall which will have a positive","impact on profitability (AO3).","• Better quality is an important part of the product component of the marketing mix and so can improve","brand appeal and loyalty, meaning those aspects could potentially be stressed within the promotional","mix (AO1). This can increase sales and market share and again help with profitability as economies","of scale can be experienced, driving down unit costs and so increasing margins (AO3).","• Strict quality standards for durability factors may not be appropriate given the fast fashion nature of","the company’s products (AO1, AO2). If SHEIN does aim for more durability from its clothes then","higher quality materials are likely to be required; this could lead to higher variable costs, reducing","gross profit margins. High quality standards may therefore increase the price point at which clothes","are sold, which may not appeal to the fast fashion market and so may reduce sales volumes and","reduce economies of scale and lead to lower profits (AO2, AO3).","• There will be costs involved in setting and implementing quality standards, training staff and in","reworking items, sending them back etc. All these additional costs may reduce profitability (AO1,","AO3).","Evaluation","• A focus on improving quality may play an important part in cutting costs and improving both the","employee and the customer experience, which can contribute to lower unit costs and higher sales","and so improve profitability. However, while certain aspects of cost measurement may be easy to","quantify, the impact of more subjective potential benefits such as those which could lead to","increased revenues is difficult to gauge (AO4).","• It depends on the costs of quality relative to the benefits it generates. Presumably SHEIN will attempt","to assess the relative costs and benefits itself and is only likely to continue with this approach if","profitability increases (AO4).","• It depends on whether the quality of SHEIN’s products ends up better than its competitors. This will","determine the impact in competitiveness and profitability (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"section":"A","marks":15,"text":"If SHEIN is seeking to launch a new range of clothing in 2026, it might consider two new suppliers: Xpress and Reliont.\nEvaluate both suppliers and recommend which one would be the better choice to produce the new range for SHEIN.\nCalculate Xpress’ expected capacity utilisation to support your answer.","kind":"evaluate","quant":true,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of factors influencing the choice of suppliers (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of SHEIN (AO2)","• to analyse the factors influencing SHEIN’s choice of supplier (AO3)","• to evaluate which is the most appropriate supplier to produce SHEIN’s new range (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 5 response should include a calculation of Xpress’ capacity utilisation and include this in a","supported judgement of which supplier to choose.","• A response may choose Xpress or Reliont. A decision may be qualified (for example, ‘it depends","on’).","Calculation","Output at Xpress with SHEIN’s order would be 12 million + 2.4 million = 14.4 million units","Capacity utilisation = (output/maximum output) x 100 = (14.4 million/15 million) x 100 = 96%","Indicative content","• The choice of supplier for a new range can be based on a range of factors such as speed of delivery,","payment terms, environmental factors and supplier/customer relationships. Note: This is not an","exhaustive list; allow other relevant factors (AO1).","• Xpress has shorter lead time which fits with the fast fashion model and should enable SHEIN to","respond more quickly to customer demand (AO1, AO2). This could help it be more competitive and","boost sales in a fast-changing industry, where getting products to market quickly can be a source of","competitive advantage (AO3). This may be a particularly relevant consideration in this market where","speed can be a key differentiating factor (AO4). Xpress is also based in China, which is where","SHEIN’s main warehouse facility is; this could reduce the complexity of establishing and operating","this new part of the supply chain (AO1, AO2). It would also significantly improve the sustainability of","this choice as reduced transport costs and emissions would be entailed. This could satisfy multiple","measures within a more sustainable approach (AO3).","• Xpress’s CO","2 emissions are significantly lower 50 000kg v 65 000kg. Given SHEIN’s commitment to","cut its total CO2 emissions when those of the industry are predicted to rise by 45% by 2030 (AO1,","AO2), this may be an important factor indicating that both companies’ objectives are well-aligned. If","SHEIN wants to live up to its stated values in relation to tackling waste and circularity at every stage,","it may feel that Xpress may offer a better option that balances the needs of customers, planet and","profitability (AO2, AO3).","• As a much smaller producer, Xpress may struggle to meet demand if SHEIN finds that items from the","new range sell well and they wish to quickly increase production levels (AO1, AO2). The capacity","utilisation is 96% (OFR) with the current order which does not give much capacity if demand","increases. It also means if there were any issues with equipment or production the lack of much","excess capacity could make it difficult to fulfil orders. This could threaten sales of new items which","are likely to be time-sensitive and issues with inventory may affect customers’ overall perceptions of","the business. SHEIN also has ambitious growth plans and so may want to ensure that it works with","supply-chain partners who are capable of matching its scale (AO2, AO3).","• Xpress has far more customers than Reliont. SHEIN may be concerned about how important its","orders are relative to other customers. These other customers might be prioritised if the supplier has","issues with capacity and needed to focus on only some of its customers (AO1, AO2). This may make","SHEIN consider Reliont where it might have more buyer power (Porter’s five forces – sophisticated","concept) and push prices down, enabling lower prices for its customers which can be important in the","highly competitive fast fashion market (AO2, AO3).","• Reliont has longer payment terms. This is better for SHEIN’s cashflow as it holds on to money for","longer (AO1, AO2). This, therefore, may be better for the liquidity of SHEIN (AO3). However, given","SHEIN’s scale this may not be a major consideration (AO4).","• Reliont has a much lower defective product rate (1.25% compared to 2.43%) (AO1, AO2). With","quality being a key consideration for SHEIN, this consideration may be important in the","decision-making process, particularly because of the use of lean production systems within the","company and the importance of price competitiveness in this market (AO2, AO3).","• Given the much larger size of Reliont, it may find it much easier to supply the volumes of clothing that","SHEIN require as this represents a much smaller proportion of their total output (AO1, AO2). It has","more spare capacity and so could be better placed to deal with increased order sizes at short notice.","A large organisation such as SHEIN may prefer to deal with a supplier that appears to be able to","supply a greater volume and therefore proportion of SHEIN’s production demands as this reduces","the complexity of managing its supply chain and may allow it to access greater economies of scale","(sophisticated concept) when placing orders (AO2, AO3).","Possible links to sophisticated concepts include:","• Porter’s five forces in relation to SHEIN’s power relative to the suppliers and the impact of this on","price, quality and services.","• The scale of production for the bigger supplier may lead to economies of scale and lower unit costs","which could get passed on in the form of lower prices.","Evaluation","• Many different factors will be considered when making decisions about the supply chain and SHEIN","will need to decide which particular aspects it values most and weight those accordingly when","drawing up its criteria for awarding the contract (AO4).","• SHEIN will clearly be interested in the data provided, however there are significant omissions in","Appendix A. Crucially, no data is given about the prices either supplier would be willing to accept","when undertaking any contract work, and this is going to have a critical role in decision-making given","the importance of cost-competitiveness for a business that has an average selling price of just £7.90","per garment (AO2, AO4).","• Xpress appears to offer faster service and better quality and is closer to SHEIN’s warehouse. These","factors would be attractive and may make this a better option (AO4).","• Given that SHEIN supports suppliers with training and investment into advanced technology, all","suppliers will be expected to drive down their defective product rates and therefore Reliont may lose","its competitive edge in this respect (AO2, AO4).","• It may be difficult to justify choosing a supplier which appears to produce less sustainably when","SHEIN is committed to reducing its footprint in this area and so Xpress may be the preferred choice","(AO2, AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5}},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"section":"B","marks":6,"text":"Netflix is very innovative in its use of digital technology.\nAnalyse one action that Netflix can take to continue to be more innovative than its competitors.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of innovation and how to protect it (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Netflix (AO2)","• to analyse one action that Netflix could take to protect its innovation to keep ahead of its rivals (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 2 response will analyse one way in which Netflix can continue to be more innovative than its","competitors.","Indicative content","• Innovation is the process of creating new ideas, methods, products, or solutions that add value or","improve upon what already exists. To protect innovation and continue to be more innovative than","competitors a business might use patents or invest in research and development (AO1).","• Netflix could invest more than competitors in research and development and/or ensure it uses its","funds effectively to develop and use digital technology effectively – for example further improving","the user experience (AO1, AO2). With more funds, it may be able to attract and retain the best","developers in the industry and try out more ideas and projects. Innovation can be expensive and full","of errors so if significant funds are available, it can help sustain more innovation than others who","may have smaller budgets (AO3).","• Netflix could protect its technology with patents to prevent others from copying and so generate","sustainable competitive advantage through product and process differentiation (AO1). A patent","provides legal protection for a number of years, and this would keep control of a new technology, for","example, linked to its digital technologies relating to streaming, although it would not stop other","businesses developing their own technologies and might therefore not mean Netflix was more","innovative than others. A patent limits imitation (assuming Netflix does show it is willing to protect","the patent in court) but does not stop others developing their own approaches (AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"section":"B","marks":6,"text":"Analyse one way in which greater diversity in its workforce might affect the quality of the content of the shows that Netflix produces.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of greater diversity in the workforce (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of the content of the shows Netflix","produces (AO2)","• to analyse one way in which greater diversity might affect the quality of the content of the shows that","Netflix produces (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","A Level 2 response must clearly link greater diversity to the quality of content. Arguments for improving","or worsening content are accepted if there is a logical chain of reasoning.","Indicative content","• Diversity occurs when there are differences among individuals in a workforce such as race,","ethnicity, gender, age, religion, disability, sexual orientation, education, socioeconomic background","and cultural perspectives. This can lead to different perspectives in many decisions and a better","understanding of these perspectives. This can improve the quality of the output of a business","(AO1).","• Netflix is recruiting from the widest possible talent pool; this should ensure it attracts and retains the","best people. In a creative industry such as content creation, the greatest range of talents bringing","innovative ideas that can then be discussed between the widest variety of colleagues in a","collaborative fashion should ensure the highest possible quality of content for its shows can be","developed (AO2, AO3).","• Operating in 190+ countries and 30 languages means that Netflix has considerable scope to adapt","its marketing mix by creating specific products (i.e. shows) for different markets. If its workforce is","as diverse as possible, it has the best possible chance of creating content that will appeal to the","specific desires of certain sub-markets and support opportunities to meet local needs (AO2, AO3).","• The Netflix aims for diversity, and this is likely to make diverse individuals feel valued, activating a","sense of belonging and so providing a powerful motivator according to Maslow. A highly motivated","workforce can support the generation of high quality, innovative output (AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"section":"B","marks":9,"text":"Assess how important the culture of Netflix is likely to be in determining its competitiveness.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of culture and its impact on competitiveness (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Netflix (AO2)","• to analyse the importance of culture in determining competitiveness (AO3)","• to evaluate the importance of culture on Netflix’s competitiveness (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 3 response must weigh up the arguments relating to culture and competitiveness. To","achieve the highest mark there must be a clear focus on the importance of culture in terms of what","Netflix offers relative to competitors.","• A response may judge that the impact is high or low. The overall judgement may be qualified.","Indicative content","• Culture refers to the values, attitudes and beliefs of employees in an organisation. Competitiveness","refers to the relative benefits of a business compared to rivals. Culture can shape the way people","behave and what they do and prioritise; this impact on decisions and attitudes and so can influence","competitiveness (AO1).","• The culture at Netflix encourages individuals to make decisions; this can lead to faster decision","making and mean that Netflix responds more quickly to any challenges or seizes opportunities more","readily, making it more competitive because what it offers or how it offers it is ahead of the","competition (AO1, AO2, AO3).","• The Netflix culture focuses on high performers; this means people need to do what they do well to","stay or to be rewarded (AO1, AO2). Where employees understand exactly what the company","requires of them, they can perform their roles to a high standard and where those expectations are","of continuously high performance it should drive standards up. This means that Netflix’s output and","customer service should be competitive, i.e. better than rivals (AO3).","• At Netflix, teams are brought together to add specific inputs to completing a project. This can","contribute to effective problem-solving and high levels of creativity which seem to fit well with","Netflix’s requirement for innovative programming and technical solutions to offer to customers (AO1,","AO2) and so could support improved competitiveness by contributing to high quality and distinctive","output (AO3).","• The highlighting of collaboration and creativity (and relatively low levels of remote working) give","clear signals as to how employees are expected to behave. This can lead to teams working","effectively because they share a common understanding at Netflix of how to move forward and run","projects, which can improve team cohesion and lead to quicker task completion and better","outcomes than if cultural symbols were weaker and teams took longer to become a real team with a","compelling direction (Hackman’s model of team effectiveness) (AO1, AO2). This can aid","competitiveness as content may be higher quality or perhaps carry more of a distinctive Netflix","brand that viewers may come to recognise and value (AO2, AO3).","• Netflix appears to have features of a strong culture, and this can create resistance to change (AO1,","AO2). This can make it very difficult for employees to accept change and so if market conditions or","demands of the roles alter dramatically, for example, when new competitors enter the market or","rivals introduce new service features, it may be difficult for Netflix to respond quickly and","appropriately because its strong culture means it takes longer to respond to competitive threats","(AO2, AO3).","• If culture becomes so entrenched that it discourages diverse perspectives from being heard, for","example, if ‘the captain of the ship’ becomes too domineering and does not encourage full","participation or becomes too authoritarian in decision-making processes, it can limit creativity.","Netflix relies on this value as a key source of competitive advantage and so any reductions in","creativity will have a dramatic impact on its competitiveness (AO2, AO3).","Evaluation","• Customers buy the product not the culture. The culture may lie behind the operations but ultimately","the competitiveness comes from what people are watching and paying for, relative to other firms","(AO2, AO4).","• Culture is difficult to imitate or copy and, in an industry where the main competitors are trying to","establish distinctive identities that promote brand loyalty, the benefits that arise from having a","well-understood and effective culture can provide a strong source of competitiveness (AO4).","• Whether competitiveness is positively (or even significantly) affected by Netflix’s culture may","depend on how well that culture supports the necessary behaviours that develop competitive","advantage. If the culture is too rigid and cannot adapt to changing requirements, lacks employee","buy-in or is not well understood, it may have little impact or a negative one (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"section":"B","marks":9,"text":"Recent strike action has delayed Activity C in the development of the new series by 6 months.\nAssess how this delay might affect Netflix’s revenue.\nCalculate the possible impact of the delay on the critical path to support your answer.","kind":"assess","quant":true,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of critical path (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Netflix (AO2)","• to analyse the impact a delay has on Netflix’s revenue (AO3)","• to evaluate the impact on Netflix’s revenue (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 3 response will include a calculation of the impact on revenue and assess the impact of","this.","• For the top of the level, the judgement should include the calculation, but it may be qualified (for","example, ‘it depends on’).","Calculation (AO1 and AO2)","As part of the demands for this question, learners are expected to make effective use of critical path","analysis and perform a calculation of the expected impact on revenue.","These would come in the form of:","• The delay of 6 months means the new critical path would be ACEH.","• Calculating the duration of that critical path as 19 months (4 months + 9 + 4 + 2 = 19 months).","• This will delay the project’s completion by 4 months (from 15 months to 19 months).","If this does occur, Netflix’s expected revenue might be expected to fall by 45%.","If a learner inaccurately calculates the impact on the critical path, then this should be taken into account","when marking their answer. Learners may still be awarded marks for carrying their error forward.","However, it should not be possible for a learner to be awarded full marks if the calculations are not","executed accurately.","Indicative content","• With the delay the critical path changes from ABDEH to ACEH (AO1, AO2).","• Activity C previously had a float time of 2 months and so the delay to C means that the earliest start","time for activities E, F and G is now 19 months rather than 15 (AO1, AO2).","• The project is likely to be delayed by a total of 4 months which will mean it cannot be screened as","scheduled for 19 months (AO2, AO3).","• According to the data provided, the deal will affect subscriptions revenue with an expected loss of","45% of a revenue of $350 million (AO2, AO3).","• With fewer subscribers, advertising revenue could also be adversely affected which would further","reduce the level of sales revenue (AO2, AO3).","• The network diagram can only ever represent an estimate of how long each activity within the project","may take and may be an over or under-estimate and therefore the impact may not be a 45% fall","(AO2, AO4).","• A production director has suggested more resources could be used. This may mean that some of the","activities can be sped up and therefore the impact of the delay offset (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Evaluation","• There is a risk of a loss of a fall of 45% of subscriptions revenue but this depends on the accuracy of","the data and whether mitigating actions can be taken to reduce the effect. The estimate was made","by a finance assistant. This may call into question the reliability of the data provided. The person is","from finance and seems relatively junior therefore may not have full insight into the operational","processes involved (AO2, AO4).","• Given the scale of the potential impact of delays on revenue, it may be decided to reduce the amount","of time devoted to any of the activities on the critical path, for example, less editing in post-production","if the impact on product quality will have a lesser impact on revenue earnings than would be caused","by failing to meet the production completion schedule (AO2, AO4).","• In the long term, the impact will depend on the quality of the show; if it is good, then even with the","delay, new revenue can be generated from re-runs and a new series in which case this loss may","seem less significant (AO2, AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"section":"B","marks":15,"text":"Evaluate two ways Netflix could increase employees’ motivation and recommend which one it should choose.","kind":"evaluate","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5","body":["Demands of the question","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of ways to motivate employees (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Netflix (AO2)","• to analyse two ways of improving employees’ motivation (AO3)","• to evaluate two ways for Netflix to increase employees’ motivation and recommend the best way","(AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 5 response must make a well supported recommendation making clear how this will","increase motivation and why it is better than the alternative.","• Allow any ways of motivating.","• The overall judgement will depend on the ways of motivating selected. The judgment may be","qualified, for example ‘it depends on’.","Indicative content","Remote working","• Remote working refers to a work arrangement where employees perform their job duties outside of","a traditional office setting. This may appeal to some employees increasing their motivation (AO1).","• Nowadays, many employees want or expect some form of remote working (AO1). Allowing more","flexibility in this respect may allow workers to reduce commuting times and save money on","transport which can be powerful influences on lifestyles. At the moment, relatively few do remote","working at Netflix so an increase in the opportunities for this could be seen as a motivating factor","(AO2, AO3).","• If staff are given a choice over remote working, some may feel that they have more autonomy and","Maslow might view this as being a very high-level motivator, potentially affording self-actualisation,","especially as the company gives significant task flexibility over how employees decide what and","how they should work (sophisticated concept) (AO1). This would fit with the culture at Netflix (AO2).","This may provide a powerful boost to activating significant improvements in employee relations and","overall levels of intrinsic motivation (AO3).","• Netflix relies on the ideas and originality of its employees and not having them work together face to","face could jeopardise communication between staff which plays a crucial role in motivating human","beings (AO1, AO2). Remote working may lead to less interaction and less sharing of ideas and","therefore less motivation for those involved (AO3).","Share ownership","• Share ownership can improve employee engagement and motivation where employees believe they","are sharing in the company’s long-term success as part owners (AO1).","• Employees who own shares are likely to experience more of a sense of connection to the","organisation as they have a stake in its ownership. This may contribute to them feeling part of the","company and increase the feeling of belonging which Maslow suggests can be motivational","(sophisticated concept) (AO1, AO3). Share ownership can be appreciated by employees as their","wealth increases, for example when the share price of Netflix rose 2020 to end of 2021. Therefore,","share ownership can act as a financial motivator for staff, but has the benefit of not affecting cash-","flow or profits directly (AO2, AO3).","• In 2022, the share price fell significantly and so the motivational impacts of share ownership may be","significantly affected at such times and may also cast doubt in employees’ minds over the benefit of","future share awards. Additionally, the company is yet to pay a dividend and so any benefit to","employees may be either very long-term (and therefore dissociated from current personal input) in","the form of future dividends, or simply ‘one-off’ if they choose to sell their shares (AO2, AO3).","Training","• Employees might benefit from training as this increases their skills. This might give them a greater","sense of job security because they feel they can do their jobs well and not be an underperformer.","There may also be more job security as technology is changing so fast that employees may value","training to keep their skills relevant in this sector (AO1, AO2). Training might also give some sense of","recognition of them as individuals needing support rather than simply being asked to leave if","performance is not high. This may motivate people who feel they are noticed (AO1, AO3).","• Training can be motivating in itself as people feel a sense of self development and potentially","Maslow’s self-actualisation (sophisticated concept) (AO1, AO2). With more training Netflix can enrich","someone’s job by making it include a wider range of fulfilling tasks and therefore giving people a","sense of development which can be motivating (AO3). Training does cost money, and Netflix may be","concerned that this could lead to people gaining training but then leaving. This would involve a loss","of the investment made. (AO1, AO2, AO3). However, Netflix seems to value talent and pays the top","rates so this may not be an issue (AO4).","Possible links to sophisticated concepts include:","Use of motivation theorists: Maslow’s hierarchy of needs and Herzberg’s two factor theory.","Evaluation","• Remote working may improve motivation if employees value more freedom over where as well as","how they choose to fulfil their responsibilities. It may allow reduced costs if the fixed costs of office","space can eventually be reduced; however, the extent to which it might increase costs of monitoring","or cause drop-offs in quality or output are more difficult to ascertain. It could also be viewed as","attempting to avoid demotivating staff rather than choosing an option because it will necessarily be","seen as a motivator (AO4).","• Share ownership may have continuing benefits as shares can be awarded and so contribute to","ongoing motivation, in a way that one-off measures such as allowing staff to work remotely may not","be able to, because once staff become accustomed to such a ‘benefit’ it may become a simple","expectation rather than a clear motivator (AO4).","• Training may enable even more empowerment, job enrichment and provide a greater sense of job","security as it helps people to become better performers. It is an investment, and the motivating","impact will depend on who gets what training opportunities and the extent to which this meets their","needs (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5}}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: SHEIN"},{"t":"p","x":"SHEIN is an online fashion retailer. It has nearly 10 000 employees, sells in over 150 countries and operates in over 20 languages."},{"t":"p","x":"SHEIN’s annual revenue target for 2025 is $58.5 billion, up from $22.7 billion in 2022."},{"t":"p","x":"That would exceed the existing combined sales of its main competitors, H&M and Zara."},{"t":"p","x":"The average price of an item sold by SHEIN is just £7.90. The company’s website has over 600 000 items on sale. It adds 6 000 new items each day."},{"t":"p","x":"SHEIN has a lean production model. It only produces 100 to 200 pieces of any item when launched and responds with increased production only if demand justifies it. The company uses computer models to try and estimate future sales accurately and match production to demand more closely than its competitors. Through its on-demand production, SHEIN is able to consistently limit the amount of excess inventory to a level that is much lower than traditional retailers."},{"t":"p","x":"The company supports its suppliers with training and investment in advanced technology."},{"t":"p","x":"SHEIN ships orders to its customers directly, mostly from one huge warehouse near Guangzhou in China. This means that deliveries to customers around the world are slower than those of some competitors who have warehouses in different countries."},{"t":"p","x":"The fast fashion business model that SHEIN adopts has been widely criticised. It has been suggested that 8% of global carbon emissions are produced by the fashion industry."},{"t":"p","x":"Fast fashion businesses are also criticised for encouraging consumers to replace, rather than repair or reuse clothing. Fast fashion allows customers to buy cheap products which they then discard, before quickly buying something new. There have also been criticisms of conditions in some of SHEIN’s suppliers’ factories in China."},{"t":"p","x":"SHEIN has now decided to focus more on environmental sustainability. It states, “Our operations are guided by a sustainability mindset tackling waste and circularity at every stage”. The company aims to optimise the efficiency of its processes to conserve water, energy and raw materials while minimising waste. In September 2022, as part of its sustainability approach, SHEIN committed to reducing absolute (total) greenhouse gas emissions by 25% by 2030, when those of the industry are predicted to rise by 45% by 2030."},{"t":"p","x":"SHEIN has strict quality standards. Its approach includes a quality-check process where products are examined when they arrive at the warehouse. Factors tested include tear-strength and whether the colour of an item will fade when washed. Departments within the business are treated as if they are customers as part of its quality assurance approach."},{"t":"p","x":"SHEIN’s values for employees include being good team-players who work well in a collaborative environment."},{"t":"p","x":"If SHEIN was seeking to launch a new range of clothing in 2026, it might consider two new suppliers: Xpress and Reliont. Appendix A shows information on these suppliers."},{"t":"p","x":"The expected orders for the product in the first year would total 2.4 million units."},{"t":"p","x":"Source: Shein, 2024"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Fictional information on two potential suppliers"},{"t":"p","x":"Xpress Reliont Location Jiangsu, China Braga, Portugal Total annual current production (before SHEIN order) (million units)"},{"t":"p","x":"12 36 Lead time (days) 9 15 Proportion of items produced with defects 2.43% 1.25% Annual maximum possible"},{"t":"tbl","x":["output (million units) 15 50","Total annual CO2 emissions","from manufacturing (kg) 50 000 65 000"]},{"t":"p","x":"Current number of customers (before SHEIN order) 10 2 Payment terms (days) 15 30"}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Netflix"},{"t":"p","x":"Netflix is one of the world's leading entertainment services. It employs around 13 000 people. The company has over 430 million shares. It has never paid a dividend. It has more than 247 million paid memberships in over 190 countries. Members enjoy TV series, films and games across a wide variety of genres and in over 30 languages. Members can play, pause and resume watching Netflix’s content as much as they want: anytime and anywhere. Netflix’s competitors include Disney+, Amazon Prime, HBO and Paramount."},{"t":"p","x":"The use of advanced digital technology affects all aspects of Netflix’s operations. For example, it:"},{"t":"li","x":["enables the smooth streaming of content","adjusts the quality of the video for the platform it is viewed on","enables the production of high-quality content","enhances users’ experience on the Netflix site through features such as efficient search functions and personalised recommendations of content."]},{"t":"p","x":"Netflix aims for diversity in its workforce. Its employees are paid top rates for what they do. Netflix claims it has a unique culture. Netflix’s leaders highlight the importance of collaboration and creativity. 3% of Netflix employees work remotely compared to a national average of 14%. There are very few rules at the business because Netflix trusts employees to make the right choices. Employees are empowered and given the freedom to decide what to do and delegation is high but, in return, must take responsibility for their actions. The company expects employees to be high performers and excellent team players. Underperformers are asked to leave. For every significant decision, Netflix identifies an “informed captain of the ship”. This person is an expert in their area and is responsible for listening to other people’s views and then making a judgment call on the right way forward."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B shows a fictional network diagram with an example of a major new series that"},{"t":"p","x":"Netflix could launch. In this example, if the series is released within 15 months, it will coincide with the peak viewing period, which could result in subscription revenue of approximately $350 million in the first year. A financial assistant has provided an estimate of how any delay might reduce this year’s income from subscriptions. A production director has also suggested that additional resources could be allocated to the project if necessary. If the series is successful, it may continue to be shown in future years and could lead to the development of a second season."},{"t":"p","x":"Source: information sourced from Netflix, 2024"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B: A fictional network diagram for potential development of a new series (activity"},{"t":"p","x":"durations in months) Activity A Planning and financing B Market research C Scriptwriting D Casting E Filming F Pre-release promotion G Screen-testing H Post-production"},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix C: Projected fictional impact of delays to the series development project"},{"t":"p","x":"Length of delay after the 15 month launch target Expected impact of the delay on this year’s subscriptions revenue from this series"},{"t":"tbl","x":["1 month –10%","2 months –20%","3 months –30%","4 months –45%","5 months –55%","6 months –70%","Appendix D: Netflix share price 2019–2023 ($)"]},{"t":"p","x":"Permission to reproduce all copyright material has been applied for. In some cases, efforts to contact copyright-holders may have been unsuccessful and AQA will be happy to rectify any omissions of acknowledgements. If you have any queries please contact the Copyright Team."},{"t":"p","x":"Where appropriate, real company data will be provided. However, in some instances, data may be fabricated solely for illustrative purposes (Appendix A, Appendix B and Appendix C). Any such fabricated data is not affiliated with, nor does it represent, any actual business or organisation."},{"t":"p","x":"($)"}]},"caseNames":["SHEIN","Netflix"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-2","title":"Sample set 1 · Paper 2"},{"id":"aqa26-sample-p3","year":"2028","paper":3,"series":"Sample assessment material (first exam 2028)","source":"aqa","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"section":"A","marks":6,"text":"Analyse one advantage to the owners of Finlay’s Farm Ltd of keeping the business small.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of one advantage of being a small business (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse one advantage to the owners of keeping the business small (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","Level 2 responses will provide a logical link between being small and an advantage to the owners of","Finlay’s Farm Ltd.","Indicative content","• Advantages of small businesses include quick decision making and easy communication with","employees. Other advantages accepted (AO1).","• Finlay’s Farm Ltd only has four employees including the two founders/owners. This may make it","easier to communicate with employees as there are less people involved in decisions (AO1, AO2)","This level of good communication may improve the sense of shared objectives and lead to less","conflict (AO3). Finlay’s Farm Ltd has a clear focus on ethical decisions; with only four people it","should be possible to share and build a common set of values and ensure that decisions are","aligned to this more easily than if the business had many potentially conflicting views (AO1, AO2).","This should help avoid the problems that large scale businesses can encounter, i.e. diseconomies","of scale due to coordination and control issues and help to compete against the bigger farms (AO3).","• The owners may see the smallness of the business as an important element of its attraction.","Customers may welcome working with a small business and dealing directly with the owners. David","and Wilma have built good long-term relationships with customers (AO1, AO2). This may help when","holding on to customers and getting repeat business. Customers may value the fact they can talk to","the person who actually makes the decisions. This might also help Finlay’s Farm Ltd with its","positioning as a niche business rather than major high-volume producer. This may support higher","prices (AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"section":"A","marks":6,"text":"Analyse one way in which a change in the legal environment might affect the profits of Finlay’s Farm Ltd.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of the legal environment and its effect on profits","(AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding in the context of Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse one way a change in the legal environment might affect Finlay’s Farm Ltd’s profits (AO3).","Markers’ guidance","• Level 2 responses will link logically a relevant change in the legal environment of Finlay’s Farms to","the effect on its profits.","• Responses might argue profits increase or decrease.","Indicative content:","• The legal environment includes laws relating to employment, consumers and trade. These laws can","restrict business behaviour and add costs which can affect profits (AO1).","• Employment laws. Finlay’s Farm Ltd employs four people who will have employment rights such as","maternity, dismissal and redundancy rights (AO1, AO2). Changes in these laws could increase","employment costs, for example, maternity pay, minimum wages, redundancy pay, and this increase","in costs could reduce profits (AO3).","• Consumer laws. Finlay’s Farm Ltd sells cheese and ice cream and so will have regulations on","labelling and how it promotes its products (AO1, AO2). Changes in the law could affect the","company’s marketing mix and could increase costs and reduce profits, for example, if it has to","change labels or find ways of producing differently to meet new standards. The additional","regulations may reduce revenue and increase costs reducing profits (AO2, AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"section":"A","marks":9,"text":"Assess the value to Finlay’s Farm Ltd of introducing Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) reporting.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG)","reporting (AO1)","• to apply knowledge and understanding of this to the context of Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse the value to Finlay’s Farm Ltd of introducing Environmental, Social and Governance","(ESG) reporting (AO3)","• to evaluate the value to Finlay’s Farm Ltd of introducing Environmental, Social and Governance","(ESG) reporting (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• Level 3 responses will have a clear supported judgement on the value of ESG reporting with a focus","on the introduction of this by Finlay’s Farm Ltd.","• A response may make a judgement that introducing ESG reporting will be very valuable to Finlay’s","Farm Ltd or that it will not be valuable. An overall judgement on the value of ESG reporting to","Finlay’s Farm Ltd may be qualified.","Indicative content","• ESG reporting involves disclosing of environmental, social and corporate governance data. Its","purpose is to highlight a company's ESG activities while improving investor transparency. It can","help a business make better decisions in relation to ESG and can attract investors (AO1).","• ESG reporting may help raise the profile and appeal of Finlay’s Farm Ltd. It may appeal to some","customers or potential investors who like this approach to reporting rather than narrower financial","reporting (AO1). Therefore, this might help with future crowdfunding or other efforts to attract","investment and may attract more visitors to the farm visitor centre (AO2, AO3).","• ESG reporting may help Finlay’s Farm Ltd to understand the impact of its actions and ensure it","meets the standards it sets itself (AO1, AO2). The business is clearly very focused on ethical and","environmental issues and therefore its owners would probably want to know how it was doing in","these areas either to confirm it was doing well or highlight areas where they could improve to","ensure the actions of the farm are positive in terms of its environmental and social impact (AO2,","AO3).","• ESG reporting may help with publicity and bookings for the owners to speak at events and win","awards (AO1, AO2). This is good for the business and helps David and Wilma promote their","messages about organic farming. This can generate more investment and visitors (AO2, AO3).","• There may be costs and time involved in auditing and producing reports, for example, if external","auditors are used, they will charge a fee for the research and writing of the report (AO1). If this","reporting covers all areas of the business (ice cream, dairy and potentially glamping) it may","therefore increase costs further as separate reports may need doing or different indicators may be","used (AO2, AO3).","• No major external investment/outside investors are involved in Finlay’s Farm Ltd and so there may","not be the need for such reports and no need to incur these costs (AO1, AO2). Those involved may","feel close enough to what is happening anyway given it is a small business and therefore not feel the","need for the additional efforts required for formal reporting (AO2, AO3)","Evaluation","The value of introducing ESG reporting depends on:","• what is measured: there are no set items to be included in an ESG report and so its value lies in","what is being measured and how useful these indicators are to Finlay’s Farm Ltd and stakeholders","(AO4)","• how it is undertaken – the value of the report will depend on how accurate it is, and the depth of","insights provided. This may be linked to who is undertaking it and their expertise. A report the farm","produces itself may potentially be of less value than an external one as they may find what they","want to find but this may be more expensive (AO4)","• who does it and what it finds and what is done in response to the findings. The value of the report","will be greater if those who receive it are open to responding and investing resources where needed","(as opposed to simply reading it but taking no action) (AO4)","• where it is published. The impact of an ESG report will depend on where it is published for example,","is it open to all stakeholders to comment on or kept within the business? If no-one except the","owners sees it, then the value may be more limited as they are less accountable (AO4)","• given that this reporting is being introduced, there may be initial challenges getting the process to","work. For example, deciding what to measure, who to use to do the report, the format of the report","and how to action recommendations. These issues may be resolved over time so the value may","change from when it is first introduced (AO2, AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"section":"A","marks":9,"text":"Assess whether diversifying into glamping would be a good decision for Finlay’s Farm Ltd.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["The demands of the question are","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of diversification (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding of this to the context of Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse the impact of diversifying into glamping for Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO3)","• to evaluate whether diversification would be a good idea for Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• .Level 3 responses will have a clear supported judgement on whether diversifying into the glamping","market would be a good decision for Finlay’s Farm Ltd.","• Responses may argue it is, or it is not, a good decision.","Indicative content","• Offering glamping involves offering new products to new markets which is an example of","diversification in the Ansoff Matrix (sophisticated concept). This brings opportunities but also risks","because so much is new for Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO1). It will be a new product and a new target","market for the business and therefore there is a risk that a loss could be made. Furthermore, the time","taken to develop this business could divert attention from the farm and have an impact on the farm’s","success (AO1, AO2, AO3)","• Entering this market can link to the farm’s focus on sustainability and environmental issues (AO1,","AO2). The farm already attracts people to learn about biodiversity and so these people may be","interested in a glamping stay which can increase business at the shop and mean the overall returns","are higher than the forecast suggests (AO2, AO3).","• Finlay’s Farm Ltd receives government agricultural grants and subsidies. It also faces more","complex exporting regulations after the UK left the European Union. In addition, a risk that the","business faces is that customers may become more price sensitive (AO2). All of these factors may","mean that the current market becomes less attractive. For example, the loss of subsidies may affect","costs. The increased complexity may mean time is taken on administration and reduce the level of","exports. The price competition may reduce profit margins. As a result, moving into glamping may be","an option to sustain profits (AO3).","• The business will need to raise £90 000 to finance the setting up of the glamping and given that it is","already highly geared this may not be easy (AO1, AO2). It will therefore need to make sure any","interest costs have been taken into account, to ensure it can make an informed decision (AO3).","• In the first year, a profit of £36 000 is forecast to be made. This is lower than the target profit for the","first year of £38 000 (AO1, AO2). This may mean Finlay’s Farm Ltd may not want to go ahead with","the glamping as the objective is not met. However, it is not much below the target figure and the","glamping market is forecast to keep growing quickly which may create longer term opportunities and","therefore justify a decision that this is a good idea (AO2, AO3, AO4).","Evaluation","• Diversification has more unknowns than expanding in the industry that the owners know already (as","shown by the Ansoff matrix theory). It may depend on the degree of risk David and Wilma are willing","to take and how much they trust the data provided (AO4).","• It may depend on the expected growth and market conditions of the dairy industry, in that this will","affect the pressure and incentives to diversify (AO4).","• The forecast calculations are based on 120 rental weeks for eight lodges. This is a relatively small","number of weeks per lodge. This forecast may be optimistic or pessimistic. The likelihood of this","outcome depends on how the forecast has been made. Over time, the number of weeks may grow","as the glamping becomes more well known and so profits may rise (AO2 and AO4).","• The data for the glamping is only for one year; to make a good decision the business may want to","forecast for a longer period to understand the long-term returns (AO4)","• The business has already diversified successfully with the farm shop and having a broader portfolio","may protect them from any long-term negative trends in any of the other business areas. By having a","range of products, this can help ensure that if there are dog products there are others such as stars","and up and coming question marks to offset these (the Boston Matrix, sophisticated concept) (AO4).","• The farm is said to be operating close to capacity. To increase scale, it would need to buy more cows","and invest in new production facilities (AO2). This means that the business needs to invest if it wants","to grow. Therefore, David and Wilma may want to consider the likely return on investment for","glamping compared to investing further in the farm to decide whether this is a good decision or not","(AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"section":"A","marks":15,"text":"Finlay’s Farm Ltd faces many risks. Evaluate two ways the business could reduce the risks it faces and recommend which one Finlay’s Farm Ltd should implement first.","kind":"evaluate","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of risk (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Finlay’s Farm Ltd (AO2)","• to analyse how Finlay’s Farm Ltd can reduce the risks it faces (AO3)","• to evaluate two ways Finlay’s Farm Ltd can reduce the risks it faces and recommend which one to","implement first (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 5 response will have clear recommendations on how the business can reduce risk.","• Accept any relevant ways of reducing risk.","• The judgement made will depend on the ways of reducing risks that are chosen. Accept any","judgement that is supported. A judgement may be qualified.","Indicative content","• Risk refers to any event or circumstance that could negatively impact the operations of a business,","its profitability, or its ability to achieve its goals. A business may try to mitigate risk in many ways","such a market research, succession planning and investment in IT systems to protect itself from","hacking (AO1).","• Market research. For the risk of a change in market conditions, the business could undertake","market research to understand its market and its customers better. By gathering information on the","market and possible developments it can forecast potential changes. For example, it could estimate","future sales using confidence intervals to forecast the future sales of ice cream or dairy products","(AO1, AO2). This would then affect finance projections such as cashflow forecasts and budgets,","and the business can adapt human resource and operations planning accordingly. If demand is","declining, for example, the business may not replace staff who leave (AO3). However, Finlay’s","Farm Ltd will need to decide how to undertake this market research – can it afford primary market","research for example, and, if not, is secondary research going to give enough specific insights?","(AO4).","• Succession planning. If there is a risk of the founders, David and Wilma, retiring then there needs to","be a succession plan (AO1, AO2). The business needs to identify the skills and experience required","for effective leadership and then ensure that suitable training or recruitment plans are in place so","that there is no gap if the founders leave. The current leaders set up the business and therefore will","have a deep insight into how things work. Sufficient time needs to be left to train others or develop a","suitable handover plan (AO2, AO3). However, whether the founders will want to do this could be","debatable; founders sometimes fail to prepare for their departure because they don’t want to think","about it (AO4).","• Invest in systems to protect and back up IT data. This could protect against the loss of data if","systems are hacked. Investment in protection might reduce the risks and threat of data being taken.","Finlay’s Farm Ltd will have customer and financial data that it may not want to be shared and made","available publicly, for example, to competitors (AO1, AO2). It will also have consumer and supplier","data which could be used to scam customers and hack their accounts. This might make the","business legally liable and damage its brand image affecting the willingness of stakeholders to work","with them in future (AO3). To protect its systems, Finlay’s Farm Ltd may want to install firewall and","backup systems (AO1). However, this can be expensive and IT systems are continually being","tested and stretched so it is likely to require ongoing investment to try and keep safe, and, even","then, there remain risks given the skills of hackers (AO4).","Possible links to sophisticated concepts include:","Opportunity costs","Evaluation","• May have to decide which risk is greatest and focus on this. For example, if the founders have no","immediate desire to retire, this may not be the priority. IT protection could probably be implemented","relatively quickly and easily so perhaps focus on this and then look at some form of market research","to understand market trends in dairy and ice cream. In deciding on what to prepare for, the business","might consider the probability of an outcome occurring and the likely impact of it if it does as used in","decision trees. (AO2, AO4).","• May not be able to protect against all risks, so it is a question of how to allocate resources best. The","business may want to consider the opportunity costs of any action (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5}},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"section":"B","marks":6,"text":"According to Porter’s five forces model, bicycles create a substitute threat for cars.\nAnalyse one action that might be taken by the UK Government that might affect the strength of this substitute threat.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of a substitute threat (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the given context (AO2)","• to analyse one action that might be taken by the UK government to affect the strength of this","substitute threat (AO3)","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 2 response will make a clear logical link between UK government action and the impact on","the strength of the substitute threat in the context given. It could be to increase or decrease the","strength of the threat.","• The response is likely to focus on the impact of government spending, taxation or legislation, but","any relevant form of government action is creditworthy.","Indicative content","• The substitute threat refers to how easy it is to switch from one product to alternatives, for example,","the ease of switching from cars to bicycles or to other forms of transport (AO1).","• If the government legislates to ban cars from certain routes or city centres this would limit the value","that cars can provide as a means of transport whereas bicycles can continue to use these areas.","(AO1, AO2). This would therefore increase the appeal of bicycles and make this a stronger","substitute threat for cars (AO3).","• If the government invests to create cycle routes, this might create more roads where cycling","becomes a possibility. This makes cycling a more realistic way of getting to work or other locations","(AO1, AO2). This therefore increases strength of the substitute threat that cycles create relative to","cars (AO3).","• If the government subsidises the purchase of bicycles (for example, to encourage people to be","healthier), this will reduce the costs of cycling (AO1, AO2) and therefore make cycles a stronger","substitute threat because they become more appealing/more affordable as a way of travelling","(AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"section":"B","marks":6,"text":"Analyse one reason why the size of bicycle retail chains might lead to a higher operating profit margin than small independent retailers.","kind":"analyse","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of an operating profit margin (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context (AO2)","• to analyse one reason why bicycle retail chains, have higher operating profit margins that","independent bicycle retailers (AO3)","Markers’ guidance","A Level 2 response will make a clear, reasoned link between the size of a large national chain","(compared to smaller, independent retailers) and operating profit margins.","Indicative content","• An operating profit margin measures operating profit relative to sales. It is a percentage. A high","operating profit margin may be due to low unit costs relative to the price (AO1).","• National chains are larger and so are likely to experience economies of scale. For example, they","may buy some types of bicycles in bulk and so benefit from lower unit costs (AO1, AO2). Lower unit","costs will increase operating profit margins assuming that the price is unchanged (AO3).","• National chains may have access to more expertise and specialists. This can lead to lower unit","costs and higher operating profit margins. For example, access to a digital marketing specialist may","enable lower click-through costs than the £7.80 Farmoor Cycles is paying (AO1, AO2). With lower","unit costs this increases the profit per sale and therefore increases the operating profit margin","(AO3).","• National chains may have more brand recognition. Customers may trust the brand and be willing to","pay more for a product because of the reassurance of the brand and for example, perhaps greater","confidence in the quality of the product or after sales service (AO1, AO2). This might therefore","enable higher prices which would increase operating profit margins assuming unit costs are the","same (AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2}},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"section":"B","marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Farmoor Cycles should invest in the new inventory system.\nCalculate the average rate of return of investing in the inventory system to support your answer.","kind":"assess","quant":true,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of average rate of return (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context (AO2)","• to analyse factors relevant to investing in the new inventory system (AO3)","• to evaluate whether to invest in the new inventory system (AO4)","Markers’ guidance","• A correct calculation of the average rate of return alone would be awarded 4 marks – bottom of","Level 2.","• A response which was solely based on the result of the average rate of return calculation, but which","included some analysis and evaluation of this result could gain a maximum of 6 marks.","• The very top mark (9 marks) will make a clear, supported decision of whether to buy the new","inventory system including a correct calculation of the average rate of return.","• Accept judgements either to purchase the new inventory system or not. Judgements may be","qualified (for example, ‘it depends on’).","Calculation (AO1 and AO2)","Total net inflow = (£50 000) + (£20 000) + £20 000 + £40 000 + £50 000= £40 000","Average return per year (£) = Total net inflow ÷ number of years = £40 000 ÷ 4 = £10 000 per year","Average Rate of Return (ARR) (%) = £10 000 ÷ £50 000 x 100 = 20%","The Average Rate of Return (ARR) on the inventory system is 20% per year","Indicative content","• The Average Rate of Return (ARR) measures the average net inflow per year as a % of the initial","investment. A high rate of return makes an investment attractive (AO1).","• ARR is given by the equation:","(Average net inflow per year ÷ initial investment) x 100. It is a % (AO1).","• The ARR for this inventory system is significantly higher than is usually expected by the business","(20% v 10%). (AO1, AO2). This is a large difference and therefore this investment should be","appealing. This suggests the business might go ahead with the inventory system (AO3, AO4).","• Borrowing costs are expected to be 8% a year and so if Farmoor Cycles can borrow the money it","can generate sufficient returns to meet these repayments and make a profit given that the 20%","return exceeds the costs to borrow the money. This would make the investment financially viable","and suggest the business should invest in it (AO3, AO4).","• However, there are 2 years of outflows, and it takes until year 4 before the business has paid off its","outgoings. If there were concerns over the timings of payments the investment may not proceed","(AO3, AO4).","Evaluation","• It is likely that the investment would be judged worthwhile. This is because the overall profitability is","so much higher than is usually required and higher than the cost of borrowing (AO4).","• However, the figures presented are those of the supplier of the equipment. Farmoor Cycles should","check these to be sure they are accurate; it is in the interest of the supplier to present favourable","numbers and so they may be optimistic (AO4).","• It depends on whether the timings of payments is a concern; is the business happy to wait until year","four to earn positive returns? (AO4).","• The returns show only 4 years of data; the impact of the investment may last longer, and this might","mean the project is more attractive (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"section":"B","marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Farmoor Cycles’ stakeholders would be likely to oppose the business becoming a private limited company.","kind":"assess","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of how stakeholders might react if the business","became a private limited company (AO1)","• to apply knowledge and understanding of this to the context of Farmoor Cycles (AO2)","• to analyse factors relevant to stakeholders’ reaction to the business becoming a private limited","company (AO3)","• to evaluate whether the stakeholders of Farmoor Cycles might oppose the business becoming a","private limited company (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 3 response will use the context of changing from a worker cooperative to a private limited","company. A judgement does not need to relate to all stakeholder groups individually but is likely to","compare and contrast one group that might oppose and a group that might not.","• Any response is accepted if supported.","• Any judgement may be qualified e.g. ‘it depends on’.","Indicative content","• A private limited company is owned by shareholders. Becoming a private limited company may","involve new investors and this may be opposed by the existing stakeholders. A stakeholder is an","individual or group affected by the activities of the business. This can include employees, the local","community, society as a whole and suppliers (AO1).","• At the moment, the business is a worker cooperative which means decisions will be taken by","employees and they will take their own interests into account. At the same time the mission and","values of the business suggest it wants to offer value for money for customers to encourage cycling","and promote socially responsible behaviour. If the business became a private limited company, it","would be owned by shareholders (AO1, AO2). The investors would be the owners but may not","directly work in the business and may have different objectives. Therefore, Farmoor Cycles may","switch to focus more on profit, and this may come at the expense of the interests of employees.","Employees will not be involved as much in decision making (AO2, AO3). This means that some","employees may oppose any suggested change to the business becoming a private limited company","and vote against it (AO4). However, others may want the benefits of bringing in investors and","welcome the financial rewards this might bring and therefore vote in favour of the change (AO4).","• Customers may not benefit if Farmoor Cycles places less focus on providing “affordable bicycles for","cyclists of all kinds”; the desire to encourage cycle ownership may be replaced by a greater profit","focus (AO1, AO2). This could lead to higher prices and generate a focus on the most profitable","segments and types of bicycles (AO2, AO3). This means customers may oppose the proposal and","could potentially boycott the business if they felt strongly enough (AO3). However, this depends. It","is possible that a greater focus on profits leads to improved customer service and greater efficiency","which could improve things for customers and therefore even if there was initially opposition this","might fade away if the service got better (AO4). What is likely to matter is the service provided and","value for money rather than the ownership structure. Most customers would have no idea, and it","may have little influence on their purchasing decisions (AO4).","• Farmoor Cycles’ desire to be socially responsible may mean that suppliers are treated well for","example, they are kept informed and paid promptly (AO1 and AO2). Under new ownership there","may be a greater focus on trying to bargain down the amount paid to suppliers and a greater desire","to pay later to improve cashflow and therefore suppliers may suffer (AO3). Suppliers might","therefore oppose the change (AO4). However, this depends on the approach and strategy of the","new owners. If, ultimately, the new owners make the business more successful than suppliers may","benefit from more orders (AO4).","Evaluation","• Overall, it will depend on who the shareholders are and what their values are. It is possible that new","investors might bring finance and experience which will help the business as a whole and therefore","this may create more jobs, more orders for suppliers and more spending in the local community","(AO4).","• In the short run employees may benefit from their income from the sale of the business and they","may keep their jobs. Over time the new owners may make changes to staffing and personnel and","there may be opposition (AO4). In general, there may be a difference between a short term and","long-term reaction - certain stakeholders may or may not oppose given their perception of what the","change would bring but then change their mind once the changes took effect (AO4).","• It will also depend on the stakeholder group being considered. Employees are likely to be worse off","in the long run as it is no longer their business and so overall there may be more opposition.","Shareholders as a stakeholder group did not exist before so they will be better off (AO4).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3}},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"section":"B","marks":15,"text":"Farmoor Cycles wants to improve the performance of the business.\nEvaluate two actions the business should take to improve its performance and recommend which one it should focus on first.\nUse information from Appendices C–G to support your answer.","kind":"evaluate","quant":false,"ms":{"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5","body":["The demands of the question are:","• to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of business performance (AO1)","• to apply this knowledge and understanding to the context of Farmoor Cycles (AO2)","• to analyse actions relevant to improving the performance of Farmoor Cycles (AO3)","• to evaluate two actions Farmoor Cycles should take to improve its performance and recommend","which one to choose first (AO4).","Markers’ guidance","• A Level 5 response will select two relevant actions given the context and data provided. It will","evaluate these actions in relation to their impact on performance and make a clearly supported","recommendation of which one should be focused on first.","• Any supported judgement on actions to be taken should be accepted.","• A recommendation may be qualified i.e. ‘it depends on’.","Indicative content","• Improve marketing. Sales revenue is falling at a time when there is still some growth in the market.","This fall in sales is contributing to falling profits (AO1, AO2). This could be linked to the marketing","activities of the business. The business has been spending less on marketing which could be","contributing to the fall in sales, not least because the industry average spending as a percentage of","revenue is much higher. The business may not be as successful as rivals at raising its profile and","making customers aware of its offering (AO3). This means an action that could be taken would be","to increase spending in marketing activities to try and reverse the decline in sales (AO4). However,","what will matter is how this marketing spend is used and whether the right groups are being","targeted in the right way. Simply spending more does not guarantee an improvement in","performance (AO4).","• How the marketing spending is being used is a concern. Farmoor spends a lower proportion of","revenue on digital marketing than the industry average; linked to this may be the fact that its online","sales are lower than the industry average (AO1, AO2). This is potentially a concern because the","growth of online sales is expected to be much higher than store sales (AO2, AO4). The fact the","business is paying so much per click relatively suggests that the quality of what is being done is","also weak and this could lead to a low return on marketing spend (AO3). Overall, an action may be","to spend more on digital sales and look to make this spending more effective for example, by better","targeting to ensure better impact and returns (AO4).","• Review human resource management. The business seems to be spending far more of its revenue","on employees than the industry average (AO2); this may be linked to the fact it has been a","cooperative and therefore wants to share the rewards more generously with staff (AO2, AO3).","However, this proportion is high and increasing at a time when revenue is falling; this is contributing","to the fall in operating profit margin and profit. The business has also increased staffing levels","despite falling revenues, it could therefore look to reduce the number of staff it has and/or the","reward available to them (AO1, AO2, AO3). Actions may include reducing wage costs and/or","employment levels (AO4). However, if the business remained a worker cooperative this would","involve the employees themselves deciding to make these changes with a negative impact on","themselves, so this is unlikely (AO2, AO4).","• Farmoor Cycles could look at its spending on training which has decreased recently. The business","seems to have used the expertise of its staff as part of its approach to compete against the larger","national chains (AO1, AO2). Lower spending on training may weaken its competitiveness as shown","by the fall in the customer service rating (AO2, AO3). This means an action may be to increase","spending on training (AO4). However, the business would want to measure the impact of this","training on costs, quality and revenue to ensure it represents value for money (AO4).","• Improve finances. Cashflow has gone from positive £125 000 to negative £5 000 (AO1, AO2) This","could be linked to the payment terms. The business offers longer payment terms to customers and","pays suppliers more quickly. Receivables days are three times payables days. This could be linked","to its desire to act socially responsibly – by offering customers longer to pay this may make it","possible more people can buy bikes which is an aim of the business. By paying suppliers quickly","this may help their cashflow which could be seen as responsible behaviour (AO2, AO3).","Nevertheless, to improve cashflow the business may want to extend the time it takes to pay","suppliers and reduce the time customers take to pay it (AO4). However, this may lose customers’","goodwill and sales because it will affect the customers’ cashflow and so may not be a desirable","action (AO4). In terms of suppliers, FarmoorCycles may not want to upset them by taking longer to","pay, and as an independent business it may not have much bargaining power and therefore this","may not be a desirable or realistic action (AO4).","Possible links to sophisticated concepts include:","Return on marketing spend.","Evaluation","Overall, the marketing actions may seem more straightforward in that some of the human resource","decisions would require employees to reduce their own rewards given that Farmoor Cycles is currently a","worker cooperative. Whatever the ownership of the business, better marketing could lead to better","returns for the owners and so this is likely to be a focus. A worker cooperative may deliberately be","choosing high returns to employees; although greater spending on training should be reviewed if this","independent store is to compete against the national chains which have been gaining market share.","Therefore, it may be that marketing is an area to focus on first (AO2 and AO4). However other relevant","arguments will be accepted.","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application, analysis and evaluation."]},"aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5}}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Finlay’s Farm Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Finlay’s Farm Ltd is a small family-owned farm in Scotland. It has a herd of 130 cows, and their milk is used to make:"},{"t":"li","x":["Cheese. This is sold both online and through various retail stores.","Ice cream. This is sold through retailers. The company’s founders are David and Wilma Finlay. They have built strong long-term relationships with its customers. There are two other employees. The dairy industry has been restructuring in recent years with bigger farms buying up smaller ones."]},{"t":"p","x":"The company:"},{"t":"li","x":["has high gearing but has also used crowdfunding to raise finance","receives government agricultural grants and subsidies","exports some of its products; although since the UK left the European Union exporting regulations have been complex."]},{"t":"p","x":"The farm is organic. Organic farming aims to ensure that it uses processes that do not harm the environment, plant health or animal welfare. This is important to David and Wilma and its other employees. For example, the cows are fed an organic and natural diet as opposed to animal feed which has been genetically modified. The company aims to reduce energy use and greenhouse emissions and reduce the use of chemicals in farming as much as it can, even if this impacts on productivity. David and Wilma have been asked to speak at a number of environmental events and have been awarded an Outstanding Achievement award for their environmentally friendly practices."},{"t":"p","x":"The company has a good brand image and charges a premium price for its products. Its customers value its niche position in the market. It is said to be operating close to capacity. To increase scale, it would need to buy more cows and invest in new production facilities."},{"t":"p","x":"The farm has a small visitor centre which:"},{"t":"li","x":["sells its products","offers food workshops such as ice cream making","organises visits for people who want to know more about organic farming and look around the farm."]},{"t":"p","x":"Analysts say the key risks facing the company are:"},{"t":"li","x":["changes in market conditions if customers become more price sensitive","the retirement of the founders","IT issues with its online sales business such as data theft."]},{"t":"p","x":"One possible growth option for the business is to open a glamping campsite."},{"t":"p","x":"Source: Information sourced from Finlay’s Farm Ltd Appendix A: Glamping."},{"t":"p","x":"Glamping is a form of camping for those who want more luxury than a traditional outdoor camping experience. It involves outdoor accommodation such as yurts, treehouses or eco-lodges (environmentally friendly huts). Glamping usually involves proper beds, electricity, private bathrooms and kitchen areas."},{"t":"li","x":["The UK glamping market had sales of approximately £350 million in 2023. It has been growing at an annual rate of around 12–15% in recent years.","Demand tends to be seasonal. Peak season: May to September.","Many glamping sites emphasise eco-friendly practices and sustainable tourism. They are often independently owned."]},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B: Forecast (fictional) financial data for the first year if Finlay’s Farm Ltd decides to set"},{"t":"p","x":"up a glamping site. Capacity 8 eco-lodges"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Forecast sales in first year 120 rental weeks of eco-lodges","Forecast profit in first year £36 000","Initial investment £90 000","Target profit for first year £38 000"]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Farmoor Cycles"},{"t":"p","x":"Farmoor Cycles is an independent bicycle retailer in the UK. It was established in Oxford in 2002 as a worker cooperative by a group of cycling enthusiasts. It now has two other shops in Birmingham and Manchester. The UK bicycle retail sector has a few large national chains that sell bikes, such as Halfords, Decathlon and Argos, as well as many small independent businesses. The entry threat to the industry is high."},{"t":"p","x":"The mission of Farmoor Cycles is to:"},{"t":"li","x":["provide affordable bicycles for cyclists of all kinds","promote the benefits of cycling for cyclists of all kinds","build a fairer society through cooperative ownership."]},{"t":"p","x":"The business states that ‘we have the passion, specialist expertise and links with the local community that customers want’."},{"t":"p","x":"It values:"},{"t":"li","x":["democracy","honesty","openness","social responsibility","caring for others. The business is considering investing in a new inventory management system (Appendix C) which has a forecast life span of 4 years. It usually aims for an average rate of return on its investments of at least 10%. The current rate of interest for taking out a bank loan is 8%."]},{"t":"p","x":"Appendix C: Investment in a new inventory management system."},{"t":"p","x":"Year Net inflows"},{"t":"tbl","x":["£000","0 (50)","1 (20)","2 20","3 40","4 50"]},{"t":"p","x":"Source: based on calculations of Farmoor Cycles’ Operations Manager using data provided by the supplier of the equipment Appendix D: Farmoor Cycles’ data."},{"t":"tbl","x":["2023 2024","Revenue £1 904 000 £1 880 000","Marketing spending (percentage of revenue) 5% 4%","Number of employees 30 32","Employee costs as percentage of revenue 25% 27%","Operating profit margins 3% 2%","Cashflow £125 000 (£5 000)","Average spending per employee on training £2 000 £750"]},{"t":"p","x":"Average time for customers to pay 6 months 6 months Average payment time to suppliers 4 weeks 3 weeks Appendix E: Farmoor Cycles’ product information."},{"t":"p","x":"Category of bike Proportion of inventory held in Farmoor Cycles’ shops Forecast average annual UK sales growth rate"},{"t":"tbl","x":["2025–2028","Road 55% -0.1%","Mountain 30% 2%","E bikes 15% 8%"]},{"t":"p","x":"Appendix F: Farmoor Cycles’ marketing data."},{"t":"tbl","x":["2024","Online sales as percentage of total sales 15%","Store sales as a percentage of total sales 85%"]},{"t":"p","x":"Spending on digital marketing as a percentage of revenue 2% Average cost per click-through from digital adverts to website £7.80 Index of customer service rating (base year 2022=100) 82 Appendix G: UK bicycle retail industry data."},{"t":"p","x":"2024 Annual growth of the UK bicycle retail market 3% Industry average marketing spending as percentage of revenue 10% Industry average employee costs as percentage of revenue 18% Operating profit margins 6% Online sales as percentage of revenue 40% Spending on digital marketing as a percentage of revenue 4% Average cost per click through from digital adverts to website £2.50"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Forecast annual growth in store sales (2024–2027) 2%","Forecast annual growth in digital sales (2024–2027) 12%","Receivables days 90 days","Payables days 30 days"]},{"t":"p","x":"Permission to reproduce all copyright material has been applied for. In some cases, efforts to contact copyright -holders may have been unsuccessful and AQA will be happy to rectify any omissions of acknowledgements. If you have any queries please contact the Copyright Team."},{"t":"p","x":"Where appropriate, real company data will be provided. However, in some instances, data may be fabricated solely for illustrative purposes (appendix A, appendix B). Any such fabricated data is not affiliated with, nor does it represent, any actual business or organisation."},{"t":"p","x":"Source B is a fictional business."}]},"caseNames":["Finlay’s Farm Ltd","Farmoor Cycles"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-3","title":"Sample set 1 · Paper 3"},{"id":"ra10-26-p1-a","paper":1,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"A","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Amara Osei has shown several entrepreneurial characteristics.\nAnalyse how one of these characteristics has contributed to the success of Lumen Skincare Ltd.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Entrepreneurial characteristics are personal features such as resilience, risk taking, passion and innovation (AO1).","Amara re-formulated her products four times in six months after a retailer rejected them - resilience (AO1, AO2). Resilience kept her trying until she found a product that sold, so Lumen could launch and grow (AO3).","Alternative: risk taker - left a secure hospital job and invested £25 000 of savings (AO1, AO2); the risk allowed her to dedicate time to the business and build the brand, leading to sales growth of 35% a year (AO3).","Credit all relevant knowledge, understanding, application and analysis. One developed chain is sufficient for Level 2."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Lumen’s moisturiser sells at about 40% more than similar own-label products.\nAnalyse one way in which Lumen’s strong brand might affect the price elasticity of demand for its products.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["A strong brand differentiates the product and builds loyalty, making demand less sensitive to price change (AO1).","72% of customers say they would be very unlikely to switch to a cheaper brand - loyal customers (AO2). Therefore demand is likely to be price inelastic (PED less than 1 in value), so a small price rise would cause a smaller percentage fall in quantity demanded (AO3).","The effect on revenue: a price rise would increase revenue. Own-label rivals may keep demand somewhat elastic (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether spending more on influencer marketing would increase Lumen’s profits.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Influencers: people who affect the buying decisions of others; targeted, credible, low cost compared with TV (AO1).","Lumen already has 240 000 followers and sales growth of 35% - evidence the method works for its target market (AO2). Greater reach → more traffic and conversion → higher sales and contribution (AO3).","Against: diminishing returns as the market becomes saturated; influencer fees; risk of negative publicity; profit depends on return on marketing spend being greater than cost (AO3).","Judgement: likely to raise profit if spending is targeted and monitored (e.g. return on marketing spend); depends on conversion and cost (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Lumen Skincare Ltd was in a strong liquidity position in 2024.\nCalculate the current ratio for 2024 to support your answer.\nGive your answer to two decimal places.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills (6 marks): Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities (AO1). Current assets = £140k + £60k + £110k = £310k (AO2). Current ratio = 310 ÷ 420 = 0.74 : 1 (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3/AO4): the ratio is below 1 and below the industry average of 1.5 - Lumen may struggle to pay short-term debts from current assets.","Further analysis: inventory (£140k) is 45% of current assets - the acid test ratio would be (310 − 140) ÷ 420 = 0.40, weaker still; but growing online businesses often have high payables because they use supplier credit; the industry figure is fictional/analyst-based and may not be comparable; trend data and cash flow are needed (AO3, AO4).","Judgement: liquidity looks weak but the sales growth and need for inventory to support expansion may explain it; the business should improve cash management (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Amara needs £400 000 to expand.\nEvaluate two sources of finance - a bank loan at 9% or selling a 25% stake to a business angel - and recommend which one Amara should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Bank loan: retains full ownership and control; interest cost £36 000 in year 1 (9% of £400 000) plus repayments; increases gearing and fixed costs; Lumen’s current ratio of 0.74 already suggests liquidity pressure, so repayments could be difficult (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Business angel: no repayment or interest; expertise and contacts for a pharmacy rollout; Amara loses 25% of ownership and profits, may lose some control; the angel will expect a return/exit (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Sophisticated concepts that can be credited: gearing, break-even, ROCE, the Ansoff matrix (market penetration into pharmacies), risk and reward (AO3).","Evaluation: weigh cost v control; consider Amara’s characteristics (risk taker, passion for independence); growth is fast so a flexible partner may help; the loan is cheaper if profits are high; recommendation must be supported (AO4). Level 5 weighs both options and gives a coherent overarching judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Harbour Roasters sells to cafés and offices and to consumers.\nAnalyse one way in which the marketing of coffee to cafés (B2B) might differ from the marketing of coffee to consumers (B2C).","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["B2B: fewer, larger buyers, relationships and negotiation, account managers; B2C: many small purchases, mass communication, branding (AO1).","Harbour sells to cafés and offices through personal selling and account management, whereas consumer sales use its website and packaging (AO2).","B2B marketing builds long-term contracts so sales are more predictable but depend on a few clients; B2C marketing needs promotion to reach many customers and drives brand awareness (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Harbour Roasters increased its dividend in 2024.\nAnalyse one reason why the increase in dividend might have caused Harbour’s share price to rise.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Share price is determined by demand and supply for shares; a higher dividend makes the shares more attractive to investors (AO1).","The dividend rose from £8.0m to £9.6m (AO2). Higher dividends increase the return for shareholders, which raises demand for shares and, with a fixed supply, pushes the price up (AO3).","Also signals confidence in future profits (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Harbour Roasters’ market research is likely to be reliable.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Quota sampling: interviewers choose respondents to fill quotas; non-random so may be biased; sample size 800 (AO1).","The sample covers under-35s and over-50s but ignores 35-50 age group and non-customers (AO2). Bias could lead to the wrong conclusion on willingness to pay a premium (AO3).","Larger/random samples and confidence intervals would improve reliability; the research was done by the company itself (origin) (AO3). 55% v 18% gap is large, so the conclusion on the age difference is probably valid (AO4).","Judgement: partly reliable but needs checking (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Harbour Roasters’ market share has increased by more than the market has grown.\nCalculate the market share for 2023 and 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Market share = company sales ÷ market sales × 100 (AO1). 2023: 62 ÷ 410 × 100 = 15.1%; 2024: 71 ÷ 436 × 100 = 16.3% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation: sales grew 14.5% while the market grew 6.3% so market share rose by 1.2 percentage points (AO3).","Evaluation: growth is better than the market, suggesting the dividend/brand strategy is working; but the market grew slowly, share is small, and supermarket own-label competition threatens further growth (AO3, AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) selling through supermarkets and (2) an online subscription service as ways for Harbour Roasters to increase its market share and recommend which one it should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Supermarkets (intermediaries): wide distribution and volume; but buyer power, lower margins, own-label competition at half the price, risk to premium/ethical brand image (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Online subscription (direct): keeps margin, personalisation using data (AI/CRM), builds retention and loyalty; customers are largely under-35 (55% pay premium for ethical beans); needs delivery and digital marketing spend; slower growth (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Sophisticated concepts: segmentation/target market, price elasticity, Boston Matrix, return on marketing spend, multichannel distribution (AO3).","Judgement: weigh market share growth v profit and brand; subscription fits the premium segment, supermarkets fit volume; recommendation with reasons (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Lumen Skincare Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Lumen Skincare Ltd was founded in 2019 by Dr Amara Osei, a pharmacist who left a secure hospital job to launch a range of fragrance-free moisturisers for sensitive skin. She funded the start-up with £25 000 of savings and worked from her spare bedroom, packing orders in the evenings. When a retailer rejected her first products, she re-formulated them four times in six months before launching online."},{"t":"p","x":"Lumen sells only through its website and has built a following of 240 000 on social media by working with skin-care influencers. Sales have grown by 35% a year. Amara has now been told by a large pharmacy chain that it would stock Lumen if she can double production. Lumen’s moisturiser sells at £24, around 40% more than similar own-label products. A recent customer survey found that 72% of buyers would be “very unlikely” to switch to a cheaper brand."},{"t":"p","x":"Amara believes the company needs £400 000 to expand. She has two sources in mind: a bank loan at 9% interest over five years, or selling a 25% stake to a business angel, Raj Mehta, who has experience of launching beauty brands."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Extracts from Lumen Skincare Ltd’s statement of financial position, 2024"},{"t":"grid","head":["","£000"],"rows":[["Inventory","140"],["Receivables","60"],["Cash","110"],["Payables due within a year","420"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Total current assets = inventory + receivables + cash. Industry average current ratio = 1.5 (fictional, from an analyst report)."}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Harbour Roasters plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Harbour Roasters plc is a UK coffee roaster whose shares are traded on the stock exchange. It sells roast and ground coffee to cafés and offices (B2B) and packs of beans to consumers through its website (B2C). In 2024 the company increased its dividend, which pushed its share price up."},{"t":"p","x":"The UK retail coffee market has grown steadily, but consumers are divided: younger drinkers want single-origin and ethically sourced beans, while older drinkers prefer familiar blends at lower prices. Harbour’s research, based on a quota sample of 800 customers, suggests that 55% of under-35s would pay a premium for ethical beans compared with 18% of over-50s. Supermarkets have launched own-label ranges that sell at about half Harbour’s prices."},{"t":"p","x":"The directors want to grow market share. They are considering two options: (1) selling Harbour’s consumer range through the large supermarket chains; or (2) developing an online subscription service with personalised coffee blends and delivery."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Harbour Roasters plc and the UK coffee market"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Harbour Roasters sales (£m)","62","71"],["UK market sales (£m)","410","436"],["Total dividends (£m)","8.0","9.6"],["Number of shares (m)","80","80"],["Share price at year end (£)","2.50","3.00"]]}]},"caseNames":["Lumen Skincare Ltd","Harbour Roasters plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-1-A","title":"RA10 Paper 1 · Set A"},{"id":"ra10-26-p2-a","paper":2,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"A","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Orbit Logistics has a tall organisational structure with narrow spans of control.\nAnalyse one way in which this structure might slow Orbit’s response to customer problems.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Tall structure: many levels of hierarchy; narrow span of control: few subordinates per manager (AO1).","Orbit has six levels and managers who supervise four or five staff; supervisors need approval to change rotas (AO2).","Information and decisions must pass up and down many layers → slower communication and decisions → delays in solving problems for retailers and slower delivery, which may harm customer satisfaction (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Jade Whitlock’s style is to “tell people what to do and check they do it”.\nAnalyse one way in which this leadership style might affect employees’ performance.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Autocratic leadership: leader makes decisions and tells employees what to do (AO1).","Warehouse staff have little say and little responsibility; Jade checks work closely (AO2).","Employees feel undervalued and stressed → lower motivation and engagement → more errors and absence/turnover → lower productivity and higher costs (AO3). In urgent, routine jobs it may give control and consistency."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the performance-related bonus based on parcels dispatched per hour has been a good way to motivate Orbit’s employees.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Taylor: money motivates; link between effort and pay (AO1). Herzberg: pay is a hygiene factor; Maslow: pay meets lower needs (AO1).","Productivity rose briefly (AO2), but errors and stress rose; bonus on speed only encourages rushing (AO3).","Employee turnover rose sharply, which suggests it did not motivate long term; non-financial factors such as training, responsibility and recognition are missing (AO3).","Judgement: short-term productivity gains outweighed by quality problems and turnover - not a good approach on its own (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the change in Orbit Logistics Ltd’s employee turnover and employee costs suggests that its human resource management is ineffective.\nCalculate the rate of employee turnover for 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Employee turnover = leavers ÷ average employees × 100 (AO1). Average employees = (480 + 520) ÷ 2 = 500; turnover = 130 ÷ 500 × 100 = 26.0% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Turnover in 2023 (72 leavers ÷ 480) = 15.0%; 2024 = 26.0%, much higher (AO3). Employee costs as a percentage of revenue rose from 15.0% to 17.5% (AO3).","Evaluation: higher turnover increases recruitment/training costs and reduces experience; but the labour market and pay near the minimum may be external causes; need benchmarks and exit-interview data (AO4). Judgement on effectiveness."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) delayering and (2) an employee development programme as ways of improving motivation at Orbit Logistics and recommend which one Jade should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Delayering: wider spans, faster decisions, empowerment (Herzberg: responsibility, achievement), lower management costs; but job losses/redundancies, overloaded managers, short-term disruption (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Development programme: training and promotion routes meet esteem and growth needs (Maslow), improve retention and skills, reduce recruitment costs; costs and time, and no guarantee staff stay after training (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Link to data: turnover of 26.0% and employee costs of 17.5% of revenue (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: Maslow, Herzberg, Hackman, transformational leadership, employee costs ratio.","Evaluation: weigh cost, speed and likely effect; delayering fits the autocratic structure problem; development addresses retention; a combined approach is possible; recommendation must be justified (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Fieldstone Foods plc has no supplier code of conduct.\nAnalyse one way in which introducing a supplier code of conduct and audits might affect Fieldstone’s reputation.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Supplier code of conduct: standards suppliers must meet, checked by audits (AO1).","The overseas supplier was accused of unsafe conditions; supermarkets expect traceable, ethical supply (AO2).","Audits would show Fieldstone acts on ethical issues → reduces risk of bad publicity → protects its reputation with supermarkets and consumers and secures contracts (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"A delivery failure stopped production for four days.\nAnalyse one way in which a supply chain disruption might reduce Fieldstone’s capacity utilisation.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Capacity utilisation: actual output ÷ maximum capacity (AO1).","With JIT there were no inventories; production stopped for four days (AO2).","Lost production reduces output relative to capacity → fixed costs spread over fewer meals → higher unit cost and lower profit (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Fieldstone Foods plc’s factory is operating efficiently.\nCalculate its capacity utilisation to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Capacity utilisation = actual output ÷ maximum capacity × 100 (AO1) = 765 ÷ 900 × 100 = 85% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation: 15% spare capacity; fixed costs are spread over fewer meals than at full capacity (AO3).","Further analysis: the supply disruption may explain the shortfall; some spare capacity helps meet short-notice orders; 85% is reasonable for a food factory; benchmarks needed (AO3, AO4). Judgement (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Fieldstone Foods plc is in a safe position in terms of break-even.\nCalculate the break-even output and the margin of safety to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Contribution per meal = £6.00 − £3.60 = £2.40 (AO1). Break-even output = 1 800 000 ÷ 2.40 = 750,000 meals (AO2). Margin of safety = 900 000 − 750,000 = 150,000 meals (17% of output) (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation: output is 20% above break-even, giving a buffer (AO3).","Evaluation: a further disruption of a few weeks could eliminate the margin of safety; costs/prices may change; supermarkets can demand lower prices (AO3, AO4). Judgement on safety (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) holding larger safety inventories and (2) investing £2m in automated packing equipment as ways of improving Fieldstone’s competitiveness and recommend which one Tomás should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Larger inventories: reduces risk of stock-outs (JIC) and protects supply to supermarkets; storage, insurance and cash costs; waste for perishable ingredients; does not address ethical concerns (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Automation: higher productivity, lower unit costs and waste, consistent quality; £2m cost, financing and payback, job losses, risk of breakdown; may help with capacity (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Use the data: break-even 750,000 meals, capacity utilisation 85%, contribution £2.40 (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: JIT v JIC, lean production, investment appraisal, supply chain management.","Judgement: the supply failure cost £1.2m - larger inventory addresses the immediate risk; automation addresses long-term cost; recommendation may be inventory first or combined; must be justified (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Orbit Logistics Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Orbit Logistics Ltd operates warehouses and delivery services for online retailers. It has a tall structure with six levels of hierarchy, and managers each supervise only four or five staff. Decisions are made at head office and passed down; warehouse supervisors say they need approval even to change a shift pattern."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2024 the company introduced a performance-related bonus based on parcels dispatched per hour. Productivity rose briefly, but errors increased and many staff complained of stress. Pay is slightly above the national living wage, and there are few training opportunities. The chief executive, Jade Whitlock, describes her style as “tell people what to do and check they do it”. Employee turnover has increased sharply."},{"t":"p","x":"Jade is considering two ways of improving motivation and retention: (1) delayering and giving supervisors authority over rotas and quality, or (2) introducing an employee development programme with training and promotion routes."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Orbit Logistics Ltd workforce data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Employees at year end","480","520"],["Employees who left during the year","72","130"],["Employee costs (£m)","14.4","17.2"],["Revenue (£m)","96.0","98.5"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Use the average of the opening and closing number of employees where an average is required. Opening employees in 2024 were 480."}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Fieldstone Foods plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Fieldstone Foods plc makes frozen ready meals in one large factory. It sells to supermarkets which place orders at short notice and demand high quality. In 2024 the factory used Just in Time (JIT) for most ingredients, but a supplier delivery failure in March stopped production for four days and cost the business £1.2m in lost sales and fines."},{"t":"p","x":"Fieldstone’s main ingredient supplier, based overseas, was accused by a newspaper of using unsafe working conditions. The operations director, Tomás Rey, says the supplier is “the only one that can meet our volumes at our price” but admits the company does not have a supplier code of conduct and has never audited the factory. Supermarket customers have said that they expect suppliers to be “fully traceable and ethical”."},{"t":"p","x":"Tomás is considering two options: (1) moving from JIT to holding larger safety inventories of ingredients; or (2) investing £2m in new automated packing equipment to raise productivity and reduce waste."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Fieldstone Foods plc operating and cost data, 2024"},{"t":"grid","head":["",""],"rows":[["Maximum capacity (000 meals per month)","900"],["Actual output (000 meals per month)","765"],["Selling price per meal","£6.00"],["Variable cost per meal","£3.60"],["Fixed costs per year","£1,800,000"],["Annual output (meals)","900,000"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Assume all output is sold."}]},"caseNames":["Orbit Logistics Ltd","Fieldstone Foods plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-2-A","title":"RA10 Paper 2 · Set A"},{"id":"ra10-26-p3-a","paper":3,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"A","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Analyse one way in which the EU’s new rules on recycled materials might affect Nova Auto Parts’ profit.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Environmental/legal regulation: laws that require businesses to meet standards (AO1).","Nova sells 60% of output to the eurozone so must comply (AO2).","Compliance requires new materials/processes → higher costs → lower profit margins unless prices rise (AO3). Could also create opportunities (recyclable brakes) (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Use Porter’s five forces to analyse one way in which buyer power affects Nova Auto Parts.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Buyer power: ability of customers to force down prices or demand better terms (AO1).","A small number of car makers account for most demand and can switch suppliers easily (AO2).","High buyer power → Nova must accept lower prices or higher quality standards → lower profit margins and weaker bargaining position (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess the likely effect on Nova Auto Parts of the fall in the value of the pound against the euro.\nCalculate the euro price of the £18 000 braking system in January and in June to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: January price in euros = £18 000 × 1.18 = €21,240; June = £18 000 × 1.05 = €18,900 (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): the euro price has fallen by €2,340 (11.0%), so the product is more price competitive in the eurozone and demand is likely to rise (if price elastic).","Counter-argument (AO3): steel components are imported from Turkey - the weaker pound raises costs; if Nova prices in pounds its revenue per sale is unchanged but if it cuts euro prices revenue in pounds falls (AO3).","Judgement: on balance likely to help exporters in the short term, but depends on price elasticity, Nova’s import costs and how long the weakness lasts (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the new trade agreement with Asia will increase Nova Auto Parts’ profit.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Trade agreement: removes tariffs, making UK goods cheaper in Asia (AO1).","Nova can sell existing braking systems to car makers in Asia at lower prices; new market → more sales and economies of scale (AO2, AO3).","Against: transport costs, competition from local suppliers, currency risk, cultural differences; time to build relationships (AO3).","Judgement depends on the size of the market, Nova’s competitiveness and costs (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) selling existing products to car makers in Asia and (2) developing lighter, recyclable brakes for electric vehicles as strategies for Nova Auto Parts and recommend which one it should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Option 1 (market development): lower risk (Ansoff), faster (12 months), lower investment (£12m), tariffs removed; profit forecast £4m; risks of new markets, competition and exchange rates; payback 3 years (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Option 2 (new product development): higher profit forecast (£7m), matches the shift to EVs, meets EU recycled-material rules, differentiation; £18m cost, 30 months, technology risk; payback about 2.6 years at maturity (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Sophisticated concepts: Ansoff matrix, product life cycle, investment appraisal (payback/ARR), five forces, strategic drift if Nova fails to adapt to EVs (AO3).","Judgement: weigh short-term returns v long-term competitiveness; consider finance and risk; Option 2 may protect long-term survival while Option 1 provides earlier cash - recommendation must be supported; challenge the fictional estimates (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Meridian Energy’s advertising campaign has been criticised as “greenwashing”.\nAnalyse one way in which the accusation of greenwashing might affect Meridian’s profits.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Greenwashing: misleading claims about environmental performance for PR (AO1).","The campaign claimed net zero by 2035 but 70% of electricity still came from gas (AO2).","Loss of trust and negative publicity → customers switch to suppliers they believe are genuine (95 000 customers lost) → lower revenue and profit; possible regulatory action (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Meridian Energy faces a new government tax on carbon emissions.\nAnalyse one way in which the tax might affect Meridian’s costs.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Government tax on carbon emissions: an environmental policy that raises the cost of polluting (AO1).","Meridian’s gas-fired power stations emit around 3.0 million tonnes (AO2).","Tax per tonne × emissions → higher costs → lower profit unless prices rise (which may lose customers) or Meridian reduces emissions (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Meridian Energy should be concerned about employees’ resistance to change.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Resistance (Kotter and Schlesinger): self-interest, misunderstanding, low tolerance, different assessments (AO1).","Power-station staff fear job losses; only 31% understand the strategy (AO2). Resistance could delay change, reduce productivity and cause leaders to lose staff (AO3).","Management can use communication, involvement, retraining and negotiation; Lewin: strengthen driving forces (customers lost, carbon tax) and weaken restraining forces (fear) (AO3).","Judgement: significant risk but manageable if addressed early (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the environmental, social and governance (ESG) data in Appendix A suggests that Meridian’s strategy is working.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["ESG: reporting on environmental, social and governance performance (AO1).","Renewable share rose from 22% to 30%, but emissions fell only 3% (3.1 → 3.0 million tonnes) and 55 000 more customers were lost (95 000 v 40 000) (AO2, AO3).","Only 31% of staff understand the strategy (social/governance weakness) (AO3).","Judgement: progress but too slow to retain customers and avoid the carbon tax; limited data and one year (AO4). Challenge the data: company-reported."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) building a wind farm financed by a new share issue and (2) acquiring Greenfield Power as strategies for Meridian and recommend which one it should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Wind farm: organic growth, control, lowers emissions and carbon tax cost, genuine ESG improvement; high cost, long construction time, share issue dilutes ownership and may reduce share price, planning/regulation (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Acquisition: fast access to renewable customers and capacity, brand credibility; purchase cost, integration and culture clash (employees already resist change), risk of overpaying (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Sophisticated concepts: Triple Bottom Line, stakeholder approach, Ansoff, Lewin, investment appraisal, economies of scale (AO3).","Judgement: given customer loss and tax, speed matters; acquisition is faster but riskier with culture; wind farm is slower but credible; recommendation justified using the context and possible challenges to the data (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Nova Auto Parts plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Nova Auto Parts plc manufactures braking systems in the UK. It sells 60% of its output to car makers in the eurozone and imports steel components from Turkey. A new trade agreement has removed tariffs on parts sold to Asia, but the EU has introduced new rules on recycled materials which may increase Nova’s costs."},{"t":"p","x":"Nova faces strong competition from low-cost manufacturers in Eastern Europe and from large rivals which supply car makers across the world. A small number of car makers account for most of the industry’s demand, and they can switch suppliers easily. Developing electric vehicles is changing demand for brake components. The pound has fallen against the euro in recent months."},{"t":"p","x":"The board is considering two strategies: (1) market development by selling its existing products to car makers in Asia; or (2) new product development of lighter, recyclable brakes for electric vehicles."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Exchange rates (euros per £1)"},{"t":"grid","head":["","January","June"],"rows":[["£ to €","1.18","1.05"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Nova sells a braking system to a eurozone car maker for £18 000. Assume the price is set in pounds."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B: Strategic options (fictional estimates)"},{"t":"grid","head":["","Option 1 Asia","Option 2 Electric brakes"],"rows":[["Investment","£12m","£18m"],["Time to first sales","12 months","30 months"],["Forecast annual profit at maturity","£4m","£7m"],["Main risk","Unfamiliar markets","Technology may not be accepted"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Meridian Energy Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Meridian Energy Ltd supplies electricity and gas to 1.2 million UK homes. It owns two gas-fired power stations and has recently been criticised by an environmental group for its “net zero by 2035” advertising campaign, which did not mention that 70% of its electricity still came from gas. Meridian says it is “committed to a just transition”."},{"t":"p","x":"The government has announced a tax on carbon emissions and customers are increasingly choosing suppliers with renewable energy. Meridian’s chief executive, Sana Qureshi, wants to change the culture and strategy of the business, but employees at the power stations fear job losses and some managers resist change. A recent survey shows that only 31% of staff understand the new strategy."},{"t":"p","x":"Meridian is considering (1) building a large wind farm financed by a new share issue, or (2) acquiring a smaller renewable energy supplier, Greenfield Power."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Meridian Energy Ltd selected data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Electricity from renewable sources","22%","30%"],["Customers lost to renewable suppliers (000)","40","95"],["Carbon emissions (million tonnes)","3.1","3.0"],["Staff who understand the new strategy","–","31%"]]}]},"caseNames":["Nova Auto Parts plc","Meridian Energy Ltd"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-3-A","title":"RA10 Paper 3 · Set A"},{"id":"ra10-26-p1-b","paper":1,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"B","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Tandem Tea is a worker co-operative.\nAnalyse one way in which being a worker co-operative might affect the speed of Tandem’s decision making.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Worker co-operative: owned and controlled by employees, who share decision making and profits (AO1).","All six members must agree before a new product is launched; two want to stay small (AO2).","Democratic decision making takes longer → slower response to market changes and delayed launches → lost sales opportunities relative to faster-moving competitors (AO3). Could improve commitment."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Tandem Tea pays growers about 15% above the market price.\nAnalyse one way in which this ethical decision might affect Tandem’s profit.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Ethical responsibilities to stakeholders (suppliers); a conflict between profit and ethics (AO1).","Higher input costs for tea leaf; mission is “fair pay for growers” (AO2).","Higher costs → lower profit margin per pack in the short run; but may increase brand loyalty and allow a higher price to customers who value ethics → higher sales (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Tandem Tea’s main blend is likely to be profitable.\nCalculate the break-even output and the margin of safety to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Contribution per pack = £8.00 − £3.20 = £4.80 (AO1). Break-even output = 96,000 ÷ 4.80 = 20,000 packs (AO2). Margin of safety = 26,000 − 20,000 = 6,000 packs, 23% of sales (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): sales are 6,000 packs above break-even; annual profit from this blend = 26,000 × £4.80 − £96,000 = £28,800.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): a margin of safety of 23% is reasonable but a fall in demand or rise in growers’ prices would reduce it; assumes all packs are sold and costs stay constant; judgement on profitability."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Tandem Tea should accept the offer from Leafline plc to buy the co-operative for £600 000.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Takeover: selling control to another business; members receive a lump sum (£600 000 ÷ 6 = £100 000 each before tax) (AO1, AO2).","For: members get a high return on their £8 000 investments; Leafline’s resources may allow growth; resolves disagreement about growth (AO3).","Against: loss of ethical mission and control, possible job losses; the price compared with annual profits (£28 800 per year from one blend) may undervalue the business (AO3).","Judgement: depends on the members’ objectives (financial v non-financial) (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) crowd funding from customers and (2) an overdraft as ways of raising £60 000 for the new machine and recommend which one Tandem should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Crowd funding: no interest or repayment of cash (vouchers instead), builds customer engagement and brand loyalty, tests demand; uncertain amount raised, time and marketing effort, cost of vouchers, no control issue (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Overdraft: flexible and quick; interest charges are high and variable, repayable on demand, not suitable for long-term investment in a machine; risk to cash flow (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: the machine is a long-term investment; the margin of safety is modest; ethical customer base suits crowd funding; Tandem’s members are cautious (AO2).","Judgement: crowd funding is better matched to a long-term asset and to the brand; overdraft acceptable only for short-term needs; recommendation must be justified; sophisticated concepts: break-even, investment appraisal, risk (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Skyline Gym segments its customers into young professionals, families and budget users.\nAnalyse one way in which this segmentation might help Skyline to increase sales.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Market segmentation: dividing a market into groups with similar characteristics, e.g. demographic and behavioural (AO1).","Skyline’s segments differ in age and usage patterns (young professionals early/late; families need flexible classes) (AO2).","Tailoring product, price and promotion to each segment (e.g. flexible family classes, early-morning classes for professionals) → better meets needs → higher membership and retention → higher sales (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Skyline Gym uses dynamic pricing.\nAnalyse one way in which dynamic pricing might affect Skyline’s capacity utilisation or revenue.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Dynamic pricing: changing prices frequently in response to demand, time or customer data (AO1).","Prices for peak classes are 20% higher while off-peak classes are cheaper (AO2).","Off-peak discounts encourage members to switch → higher utilisation of classes at quiet times; higher peak prices raise revenue from those who will pay, or reduce overcrowding (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the price rise in one Skyline club has increased the club’s revenue.\nCalculate the price elasticity of demand to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: % change in price = (44 − 40) ÷ 40 × 100 = +10%; % change in quantity = (4,300 − 5,000) ÷ 5,000 × 100 = −14%; PED = −14 ÷ 10 = -1.4 (AO1, AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): demand is price elastic (greater than 1 in value) so a price rise reduces revenue: £200,000 before and £189,200 after, a fall of £10,800.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): the competitor’s price cut makes demand more elastic; revenue fell but variable costs may also fall with fewer members, so profit effect may differ; long-term effect and other factors need to be considered."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess the likely effect on Skyline Gym’s profits of the competitor’s 10% price cut and free-month offer.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Competition-based pricing; competitor actions affect demand (AO1).","Price-sensitive “budget users” may switch to PureFit; demand for Skyline is elastic (PED about −1.4) (AO2, AO3).","Skyline could lose members (and revenue); fixed costs of clubs stay the same so profit falls more; or Skyline could respond with matching offers and reduce margin (AO3).","Judgement: significant threat to profit unless Skyline differentiates (app, classes) (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) using AI to personalise workout plans and send reminders and (2) a loyalty rewards scheme as ways of improving retention and profit at Skyline Gym and recommend which one to choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["AI personalisation: uses app data (CRM/analytics) to tailor plans and target at-risk members; could raise engagement and retention at relatively low marginal cost; set-up costs, data skills, privacy (GDPR, cookies) and ethical concerns (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Loyalty scheme: rewards frequent visits; simple and understood; but discounts reduce margin, mainly rewards members who would stay anyway, and may attract “budget users” (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: one in three January joiners cancel; competitor price cut; PED shows members are price-sensitive; app already collects data (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: PED, segmentation, dynamic pricing, CRM, return on marketing spend (AO3).","Judgement: the AI approach targets the cause of cancellations without cutting price; loyalty discounts weaken margins; recommendation with a supported justification and discussion of risks (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Tandem Tea Co-operative"},{"t":"p","x":"Tandem Tea is a worker co-operative set up in 2016 by six friends who each invested £8 000. Every member has one vote, profits are shared equally after money has been set aside for investment, and members elect a board of three. The co-operative blends and sells loose-leaf tea through its website and weekend markets. Its mission is “fair pay for growers and a fair price for drinkers”."},{"t":"p","x":"Demand for loose-leaf tea has grown, but Tandem’s growth has been slow because decisions take a long time: all six members must agree before any new product is launched, and two members want to keep the business small. The co-operative buys from small growers at prices about 15% above the market price. A competitor, Leafline plc, has offered to buy Tandem for £600 000."},{"t":"p","x":"The members are considering ways to raise £60 000 to buy a larger blending machine: crowd funding from customers (who would receive tea vouchers) or an overdraft from their bank."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Annual data for Tandem’s main blend"},{"t":"grid","head":["",""],"rows":[["Selling price per 100 g pack","£8.00"],["Variable cost per pack","£3.20"],["Fixed costs per year","£96,000"],["Packs sold per year","26,000"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Skyline Gym Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Skyline Gym Ltd operates 14 fitness clubs in the Midlands and an app which gives members access to online classes. Members pay a monthly fee, and the company uses data from its app to see how often each member visits. About one in three members who join in January cancel by June."},{"t":"p","x":"Skyline has segmented its members into three groups: “young professionals” aged 22–34 who use the gym early morning or evening, “families” who want flexible class times, and “budget users” who want the cheapest membership. The company has introduced dynamic pricing: prices for peak-time classes are 20% higher, off-peak classes are cheaper. A competitor, PureFit, has cut prices by 10% and offers a free month for new members."},{"t":"p","x":"Skyline’s managers are considering two ways of improving retention and profit: (1) using artificial intelligence (AI) to personalise workout plans and send reminders to members who are at risk of cancelling, or (2) running a loyalty rewards scheme with discounts for members who visit regularly."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Effect of Skyline’s price rise in one club"},{"t":"grid","head":["","Before","After"],"rows":[["Monthly membership price","£40.00","£44.00"],["Number of members","5,000","4,300"]]}]},"caseNames":["Tandem Tea Co-operative","Skyline Gym Ltd"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-1-B","title":"RA10 Paper 1 · Set B"},{"id":"ra10-26-p2-b","paper":2,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"B","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Kestrel Care Agency employs most of its care workers on zero-hours contracts.\nAnalyse one way in which zero-hours contracts might benefit Kestrel.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Zero-hours contract: no guaranteed hours; employee paid only for hours worked (AO1).","Demand for home care is unpredictable and the agency has little head office staff (AO2).","Allows the agency to match staff hours to demand → lower labour costs when demand is low and flexibility when demand is high → improves profit margins (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Kestrel Care Agency does not carry out employee appraisals.\nAnalyse one way in which introducing appraisals might benefit Kestrel.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Appraisal: reviewing performance, giving feedback and identifying development needs (AO1).","Care workers say they feel undervalued and receive no feedback (AO2).","Appraisals recognise good work and identify training needs → higher motivation and skills → better quality of care and lower turnover → higher client satisfaction and lower recruitment costs (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the change in labour productivity suggests that Kestrel Care Agency’s human resource management is effective.\nCalculate labour productivity (revenue per care worker) for 2023 and 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Labour productivity = revenue ÷ number of employees (AO1). 2023: £1,540,000 ÷ 55 = £28,000; 2024: £1,715,000 ÷ 61 = £28,115 (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation: productivity rose by only 0.4% (AO3), but turnover rose from 28% to 47% and client satisfaction fell from 91% to 78% (AO3).","Evaluation (AO4): revenue per worker rose slightly because the new council contract raised revenue, which hides underlying problems; high turnover suggests management is not effective; revenue per worker ignores quality and hours worked."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the trade union is likely to improve employer–employee relations at Kestrel Care Agency.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Trade union: collective representation; negotiates pay and conditions; employee voice (AO1).","Staff feel undervalued and have short-notice schedule changes; zero-hours contracts (AO2).","A union could negotiate guaranteed hours and notice periods → improve morale and reduce turnover; but may cause conflict, reduce flexibility and raise costs (AO3).","Judgement: if management communicates and listens relations may improve (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) guaranteed-hours contracts with appraisal and training and (2) a wellbeing programme as ways of retaining care workers and recommend which one Naomi should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Guaranteed hours with training: greater security (Maslow safety needs, Herzberg hygiene), appraisal and development (motivators), lower turnover and recruitment costs, better quality; loses flexibility and may raise fixed costs (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Wellbeing programme: addresses stress and scheduling; counselling and flexible rotas reduce absence and improve engagement; costs; may not address pay/security reasons for leaving (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: turnover 47%; client satisfaction 78%; agency competes with a rival offering guaranteed hours and training (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: Herzberg, Maslow, flexible employment, employee costs, employee wellbeing (AO3).","Judgement: option 1 addresses the main reason for leaving; option 2 supports it; recommendation with reasoning about costs and likely impact (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Greenfield Packaging inspects finished products at the end of the production line.\nAnalyse one way in which moving from quality control to quality assurance might benefit Greenfield.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Quality control: inspecting output to find defects; quality assurance: preventing defects by building quality into every stage (AO1).","At present 6% of output is rejected after production (AO2).","QA identifies faults earlier → less waste and rework → lower costs and fewer rejected orders → protects customer relationships (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Greenfield Packaging is introducing quality circles.\nAnalyse one way in which quality circles might improve Greenfield’s productivity.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Quality circles: small groups of employees who meet to solve quality problems (AO1).","Defect rate of 6% and customer complaints (AO2).","Workers on the production line identify and fix causes of defects → fewer defects and less waste → more good output per employee → higher productivity; employees may feel more motivated (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Greenfield Packaging’s lean inventory management has been successful.\nCalculate inventory turnover for the year to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Inventory turnover = cost of sales ÷ average inventory (AO1). Average inventory = (£14m + £9m) ÷ 2 = £11.5m; inventory turnover = £63m ÷ £11.5m = 5.5 times (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): inventory is turned over about 5.5 times a year, so on average stock is held for about 67 days; inventory fell from £14m to £9m, releasing cash and cutting storage costs.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): lower inventory improves cash flow but increases risk of stock-outs, and benchmark data are needed; success depends on whether customer orders are met on time."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the new production line can be ready in 12 weeks.\nUse the network information in Appendix A to determine the critical path and the total project time.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Network analysis: A (4) → B (2) → D (3) → F (1) = 10 weeks; A (4) → C (6) → D (3) → F (1) = 14 weeks; A (4) → E (4) → F (1) = 9 weeks. Critical path A, C, D, F = 14 weeks (AO1, AO2). Float: B has 4 weeks, E has 5 weeks (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): the project takes 14 weeks, not 12; to finish in 12 weeks the critical path must be shortened by 2 weeks (e.g. more workers to prepare the site).","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): crashing C costs more money and may reduce quality; B and E have float; delays on critical activities delay the project; durations are estimates."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) introducing Total Quality Management with quality circles and (2) investing £4.5m in a recycling plant as ways of improving Greenfield’s competitiveness and recommend which one Karl should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["TQM: reduces defects (6%), waste and customer complaints; improves culture and engagement; training, time and benefits take time to appear (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Recycling plant: meets customer demand for recycled materials, reduces environmental impact and possibly raw material costs; high cost (£4.5m) and payback, disruption to operations (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: customers threaten to leave over carbon footprint and defects (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: TQM, lean production, circularity, investment appraisal, competitive advantage, sustainability (AO3).","Judgement: weigh cost, time, likely impact on customers; TQM is cheaper and quicker; recycling is strategically important; recommendation could be phased; justify (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Kestrel Care Agency"},{"t":"p","x":"Kestrel Care Agency provides care assistants to supply care at home for older people. It employs 61 care workers, most of whom are on zero-hours contracts and are paid only for hours they work. The agency has no head office staff other than a manager and two administrators. Managers say that “zero-hours contracts give us the flexibility to cope with unpredictable demand”."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2024 the agency won a large contract from the local council. However, many care workers have left to work for a rival offering guaranteed hours and training. The agency does not carry out appraisals and care workers say they feel undervalued and receive no feedback. A trade union has begun recruiting among Kestrel’s staff and an employee wellbeing survey showed high levels of stress and frequent short-notice schedule changes."},{"t":"p","x":"The managing director, Naomi Carter, is considering two options to retain staff and improve performance: (1) offering guaranteed-hours contracts and a structured appraisal and training programme; or (2) introducing a wellbeing programme (counselling service and flexible scheduling)."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Kestrel Care Agency performance data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Number of care workers at year end","55","61"],["Revenue (£)","1,540,000","1,715,000"],["Care worker turnover","28%","47%"],["Client satisfaction rating","91%","78%"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Greenfield Packaging plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Greenfield Packaging plc makes cardboard and plastic packaging for food manufacturers. In recent years its customers have demanded recycled and recyclable materials, and several have threatened to move their orders unless Greenfield reduces defects and its carbon footprint. The company currently inspects finished products at the end of the production line and about 6% of output is rejected as defective."},{"t":"p","x":"The operations director, Karl Brandt, wants to improve quality and reduce waste. He is considering introducing Total Quality Management (TQM) with quality circles, or investing £4.5m in a new recycling plant which would allow Greenfield to use recycled fibre for most of its cardboard. Greenfield’s inventory levels have been reduced under a lean programme and Karl is also planning a new production line, which requires a number of activities to be completed in sequence (Appendix A)."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Activities for building the new production line (weeks)"},{"t":"grid","head":["Activity","Duration","Must follow"],"rows":[["A  Design layout","4","–"],["B  Order machines","2","A"],["C  Prepare site","6","A"],["D  Install machines","3","B, C"],["E  Train staff","4","A"],["F  Test run","1","D, E"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Inventory: cost of sales £63m; opening inventory £14m; closing inventory £9m."}]},"caseNames":["Kestrel Care Agency","Greenfield Packaging plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-2-B","title":"RA10 Paper 2 · Set B"},{"id":"ra10-26-p3-b","paper":3,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"B","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"The Bank of England has increased interest rates.\nAnalyse one way in which higher interest rates might affect Aurora Airlines’ profits.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Interest rates: the cost of borrowing and reward for saving; monetary policy (AO1).","Aurora has debts of £310m and gearing of 62% (AO2).","Higher rates → higher interest payments on variable-rate debts → higher costs and lower profit; and consumers have less disposable income to spend on holidays → lower demand (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Aurora faces a new government tax on short flights.\nAnalyse one way in which this tax might affect Aurora’s competitiveness.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Government tax: an increase in costs imposed by government policy (AO1).","Aurora flies short-haul routes and competes on price with low-cost rivals (AO2).","Tax raises costs → either higher ticket prices (loses price-sensitive passengers to rivals or to rail) or lower margins → weaker competitive position (AO3). Airlines with longer routes less affected."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess the strength of the threat of new entrants to Aurora Airlines using Porter’s five forces.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Entry threat: how easily new firms can enter the market; barriers to entry such as capital, brand, regulation (AO1).","Aircraft can be leased, passengers compare prices online and switch easily → barriers are low (AO2). New entrants could cut prices and reduce Aurora’s market share and margins (AO3).","Counter: airport slots, safety regulation, brand and customer loyalty offer some protection; Aurora’s existing network (AO3).","Judgement: threat is high; Aurora should differentiate on punctuality and service (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the long-haul route to the Caribbean is a sensible strategy for Aurora Airlines.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Ansoff: market development/new product; higher margins from long-haul (AO1).","Higher ticket prices and £9m forecast annual profit (AO2); but Aurora’s gearing is 62%, fuel and carbon costs, unproven demand and no long-haul experience (AO3).","Aurora’s existing problems with punctuality and old aircraft may undermine a new route; leased aircraft reduce investment risk (AO3).","Judgement: depends on financial strength and risk appetite (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) buying 12 new aircraft for £90m and (2) the long-haul route and recommend which one Aurora should choose.\nUse the investment appraisal data in Appendix A (and Appendix B) in your answer.\nCalculate the net present value of buying the new aircraft to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: NPV = Σ(cash flow × discount factor) − initial cost (AO1). PVs: 18 × 0.909 = 16.4; 26 × 0.826 = 21.5; 30 × 0.751 = 22.5; 30 × 0.683 = 20.5; 28 × 0.621 = 17.4; total = 98.2; NPV = 98.2 − 90 = £8.2m (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): NPV is positive; payback = 3.53 years (about 3.5 years) which meets the 4-year criterion.","New aircraft: improves punctuality, fuel efficiency and customer satisfaction, helps with carbon tax; large investment financed with debt would raise gearing above 62% and interest costs (AO2, AO3).","Long-haul: higher margins and diversification; lower investment (£25m) with leased aircraft; but demand unproven, fuel and carbon tax risk, and does not fix the core fleet problem (AO2, AO3).","Evaluation: assumptions in the forecasts (rates, tax); consider discount rate, risk and finance; judgement based on NPV, payback and strategic fit; challenge the data (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Priya Nandakumar believes Cobalt has suffered strategic drift.\nAnalyse one reason why Cobalt’s strategy might have drifted from the needs of its market.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Strategic drift: a business’s strategy changes more slowly than its environment, so the gap widens (AO1).","Customers moved to cloud-based services with AI while Cobalt stayed with a desktop product; market share fell from 34% to 21% (AO2).","Past success led to complacency and slow decisions → Cobalt failed to respond to technological change → lost customers → lower revenue and profit (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Cobalt has no contingency plan for a data breach.\nAnalyse one way in which a data breach could affect Cobalt’s reputation.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Contingency plan: an arrangement to respond to an unexpected event; cybersecurity and reputational risk (AO1).","Cobalt holds sensitive financial data for small businesses and has faced an attempted ransomware attack (AO2).","A breach would expose customer data → loss of trust and negative publicity → customers switch to rivals; legal action; lower sales (AO3). A plan limits damage."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Cobalt Software plc has managed risk effectively.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Types of risk: strategic, cybersecurity, compliance, operational, financial; ways of managing: contingency/crisis planning, succession planning, insurance, investment and training, cybersecurity measures (AO1).","Cobalt has identified risks but has no succession plan for the CTO and no data breach plan; lost developers; legal case about data (AO2).","Poor planning leaves Cobalt exposed to loss of key knowledge, fines and reputation damage; the risk review itself is a positive step (AO3).","Judgement: risk identification is good but management is weak and incomplete (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Cobalt’s profit performance supports the view that its strategy has failed.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Operating profit margin: 2021: 15.5 ÷ 74 = 20.9%; 2024: 6.2 ÷ 62 = 10.0% (AO2). Revenue fell by 16%; market share fell from 34% to 21% (AO2, AO3).","Evidence of failure: falling revenue, market share and margins; staff understanding of strategy fell from 62% to 38% (AO3).","Counter: still profitable; company-reported data covers only two years; market conditions and one-off costs (ransomware, legal) may explain some of the change (AO3, AO4).","Judgement: strategy has not kept pace with the market but business is not beyond recovery (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) acquiring Linq Labs for £40m and (2) developing a cloud product in-house and recommend which one Cobalt should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Acquisition: fast access to AI technology and talent; immediate product; high cost (£40m is 6.5 times operating profit); integration and culture clash; risk of overpaying (AO1, AO2, AO3).","In-house: lower cost (£22m), control and builds Cobalt’s own skills; three years is slow while market share is falling; risk of failure and loss of developers (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: strategic drift and falling share; limited cash (profit £6.2m); contingency and succession weaknesses; resistance to change (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: Ansoff (new product development), Lewin, Kotter and Schlesinger, agile business, risk management, investment appraisal (AO3).","Judgement: speed of response v cost and risk; the answer depends on finance and the ability to integrate; recommendation with justification (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Aurora Airlines plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Aurora Airlines plc is a UK airline flying short-haul routes across Europe. Fuel costs and airport charges have risen and the Bank of England has increased interest rates three times in two years. Aurora has debts of £310m and gearing of 62%. The government has announced a tax on short flights and is considering banning flights where there is a train alternative of under four hours."},{"t":"p","x":"Competition is intense. Low-cost rivals can enter the market easily because aircraft can be leased, and passengers compare prices online and switch easily. Aurora’s strategy is to compete on punctuality and service, but customer satisfaction has fallen because of delays and cancellations caused by staff shortages and old aircraft. Its chief executive, Dan Okoro, wants to modernise the fleet."},{"t":"p","x":"The board is considering two strategic options: (1) buying 12 new fuel-efficient aircraft for £90m, or (2) a new long-haul route to the Caribbean using leased aircraft, which targets higher-priced tickets."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Forecast net cash flows from buying 12 new aircraft (£m)"},{"t":"grid","head":["Initial cost","Year 1","Year 2","Year 3","Year 4","Year 5"],"rows":[["90","18","26","30","30","28"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Discount factors at 10%: Year 1 0.909; Year 2 0.826; Year 3 0.751; Year 4 0.683; Year 5 0.621. Aurora’s payback criterion is 4 years."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B: Long-haul option (fictional estimates)"},{"t":"grid","head":["","Value"],"rows":[["Investment (marketing, crew, leases) in year 1","£25m"],["Forecast annual profit from year 3","£9m"],["Main risks","Unproven demand; fuel costs; carbon tax on long flights"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Cobalt Software plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Cobalt Software plc develops accounting software for small businesses. For 15 years its desktop product was the market leader but in the past three years customers have moved to cloud-based services with artificial intelligence (AI) features, and Cobalt’s market share has fallen from 34% to 21%. Chief executive Priya Nandakumar believes the company has suffered strategic drift."},{"t":"p","x":"A recent risk review identified a number of threats: an attempted ransomware attack on its servers, the loss of two senior developers to a rival, a legal case about the use of customers’ data to train its AI tools, and a planned rise in employer National Insurance. The board has no succession plan for the chief technology officer, who is 63, and does not have a contingency plan for a data breach."},{"t":"p","x":"Cobalt’s directors are considering two ways of responding: (1) acquiring AI start-up Linq Labs for £40m, or (2) developing a cloud product in-house over three years at a cost of £22m."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Cobalt Software plc market and financial data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2021","2024"],"rows":[["Cobalt market share","34%","21%"],["Revenue (£m)","74","62"],["Operating profit (£m)","15.5","6.2"],["Staff who say they understand the strategy","62%","38%"]]}]},"caseNames":["Aurora Airlines plc","Cobalt Software plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-3-B","title":"RA10 Paper 3 · Set B"},{"id":"ra10-26-p1-c","paper":1,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"C","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Marisol Vega kept her job as a chef for two years while building Savora.\nAnalyse one way in which this decision shows an entrepreneurial characteristic that contributed to the success of Savora Sauces Ltd.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Entrepreneurial characteristics: risk taker, resilient, focused, passionate, innovative, adaptable (AO1).","Marisol saved every week and worked for two years before leaving - focus/resilience; a careful risk-taker who managed risk (AO1, AO2).","Focus and persistence allowed her to build a customer base and finance without debt → stronger business at launch → growth to 900 outlets (AO3). Other characteristic, e.g. adaptable, can be credited."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Demand for premium sauces has an income elasticity of demand of +1.6.\nAnalyse one way in which an economic recession might affect the sales of Savora’s premium sauces.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Positive income elasticity of demand of +1.6 means a luxury/normal good: demand rises more than proportionally as income rises (AO1).","Savora’s sauces are priced 30% above own-label products (AO2).","In a recession incomes fall → a 5% fall in income would reduce demand by about 8% (1.6 × 5) → lower sales and revenue → customers trade down to own-label (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Savora Sauces Ltd is making effective use of the capital invested in the business.\nCalculate the return on capital employed (ROCE) for 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: ROCE = operating profit ÷ capital employed × 100 (AO1). Capital employed = £3.1m + £5.9m = £9.0m; ROCE = 2.4 ÷ 9.0 × 100 = 26.7% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): for every £1 of capital employed Savora makes about 27p operating profit; gearing is 66% so the business is highly geared.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): a ROCE well above typical borrowing costs of 8–9% suggests capital is used effectively, but gearing is high so risk is high; compare with competitors and over time; single-year figure; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Savora Sauces Ltd should continue to follow a premium pricing strategy.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Premium pricing: setting a higher price than competitors, supported by brand and quality (AO1).","Savora’s sauces are 30% above own-label; income elasticity +1.6 shows demand is sensitive to income (AO2). Premium pricing gives higher margins and brand image (AO3).","Risks: price-sensitive customers may trade down in a recession; supermarkets may promote own-label (AO3).","Judgement: viable if Savora keeps differentiation and targets less price-sensitive segments (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) a sale and leaseback of its existing factory and (2) selling a 30% stake to the private equity firm as ways of raising £2.5m and recommend which one Savora should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Sale and leaseback: releases £2.5m without new shareholders or new debt; keeps use of the factory; but pays rent permanently (reducing operating profit of £2.4m), loses ownership of an asset that may increase in value, long-term commitment (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Private equity: no repayments or rent; Crestline brings expertise and contacts for exports; but Marisol loses 30% of profits and control (board seat); investors expect a high return and exit (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: gearing 66% is already high (so more debt-like commitments such as rent are risky); ROCE 26.7%; Marisol’s independence and passion (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: gearing, ROCE, opportunity cost, risk and reward (AO3).","Judgement: weigh cost, control and risk; recommendation (e.g. private equity because of gearing, or leaseback for control) must be justified (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Some of Parkline’s customers buy used furniture on C2C platforms.\nAnalyse one way in which the growth of C2C markets might affect Parkline’s sales.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["C2C market: consumers sell to other consumers, often via an online platform (AO1).","Used furniture on C2C platforms is cheaper than Parkline’s new furniture (AO2).","Some customers substitute used for new → lower demand for Parkline’s products → lower sales and profit; may force price cuts (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Parkline’s share price fell by 25% in 2024.\nAnalyse one way in which a fall in the share price might affect Parkline plc.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Share price and market capitalisation (AO1); share price affects the value of the business and shareholders’ wealth.","Market capitalisation fell from £360m to £270m (AO2).","Lower value → business is more vulnerable to takeover and finds it harder/costlier to raise money by issuing shares; shareholders and directors under pressure to change strategy (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Parkline plc’s dividend policy is attractive to shareholders.\nCalculate the dividend yield for 2023 and 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Dividend per share = £22.5m ÷ 150m = £0.15; dividend yield = dividend per share ÷ share price × 100 (AO1). 2023: 0.15 ÷ 2.40 × 100 = 6.2%; 2024: 0.15 ÷ 1.80 × 100 = 8.3% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): the yield rose from 6.2% to 8.3% because the share price fell, not because the dividend rose; the dividend is unchanged.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): a high yield can be attractive but may signal investors expect the dividend to be cut; paying £22.5m while profit falls reduces money for investment; judgement on attractiveness to different shareholders."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Parkline’s marketing spend in 2024 was effective.\nUse the data in Appendix A to calculate the return on marketing spend.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Return on marketing spend = additional gross profit ÷ marketing spend (AO1) = 21.0 ÷ 14.0 = 1.5 (£1.50 for each £1), or net return = (21 − 14) ÷ 14 = 50% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): marketing generated more gross profit than it cost, so it appears effective.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): sales were still falling, so it may have prevented larger falls; the estimate comes from an agency/may not isolate other factors; short-term only; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) investing £60m in the website, delivery and app and (2) opening 12 city-centre stores as ways of improving Parkline’s performance and recommend which one it should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Digital investment: online sales are 18% and growing market share; direct distribution keeps margin, data for personalisation (CRM, analytics), supports multi-channel; high cost, competition from big online rivals, delivery costs and returns of furniture (AO1, AO2, AO3).","City-centre stores: new target markets and brand visibility; smaller stores lower risk; but rents are high, footfall uncertain, and the sales trend is towards online (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: falling share price and sales; dividend of £22.5m limits investment; marketing return 1.5 (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: multi-channel distribution, return on investment, Ansoff, price elasticity (AO3).","Judgement: weigh cost, risk and alignment to customer trends; recommendation must be justified and could combine; challenge the data (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Savora Sauces Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Savora Sauces Ltd was started in 2015 by Marisol Vega, who began by selling hot sauces made in her kitchen at street-food markets. She had no business qualifications and was told by several banks that her idea was “too small”. She kept her job as a chef for two years, saving every week, before leaving to run Savora full-time. The business now supplies 900 shops and restaurants and employs 28 staff."},{"t":"p","x":"Marisol has described herself as “driven by the product, not the money”. She is adaptable: when supermarkets rejected her original recipes as too spicy, she created a milder range which now accounts for 60% of sales. Savora’s sauces are priced 30% above supermarket own-label products. A survey by a trade body showed that demand for premium sauces has an income elasticity of demand of +1.6 whereas demand for basic table sauces is −0.3."},{"t":"p","x":"To meet a large export order, Savora needs £2.5m to build a new factory. The two options are: a sale and leaseback of its existing factory (valued at £2.5m), or selling a 30% stake to a private equity firm, Crestline Capital, which wants a seat on the board."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Extracts from Savora Sauces Ltd’s financial statements, 2024 (£m)"},{"t":"grid","head":["","£m"],"rows":[["Revenue","24.5"],["Operating profit","2.4"],["Total equity","3.1"],["Non-current liabilities","5.9"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Capital employed = total equity + non-current liabilities."}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Parkline Retail plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Parkline Retail plc sells homewares and furniture through 90 out-of-town stores and a website that accounts for 18% of its sales. The company’s sales have been falling as customers buy more online and from consumer-to-consumer (C2C) platforms where used furniture is traded. Its own-label ranges have been successful, but its branded products, supplied by large manufacturers, are heavily discounted by online rivals."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2024 Parkline’s share price fell by 25% after the company reported lower profits. Total dividends were maintained at £22.5m. Shareholders have asked the board to explain its strategy. The company has asked a market research agency to estimate the return on its marketing spend, which was £14m in 2024 and produced additional gross profit of £21m."},{"t":"p","x":"The directors have two proposals: (1) invest £60m in upgrading the website, delivery and a mobile app, or (2) open 12 smaller “city-centre” stores in cities where Parkline currently has no presence."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Parkline Retail plc share and marketing data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Share price at year end (£)","2.40","1.80"],["Number of shares (m)","150","150"],["Total dividends (£m)","22.5","22.5"],["Marketing spend (£m)","","14.0"],["Additional gross profit from marketing (£m)","","21.0"]]}]},"caseNames":["Savora Sauces Ltd","Parkline Retail plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-1-C","title":"RA10 Paper 1 · Set C"},{"id":"ra10-26-p2-c","paper":2,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"C","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Edmund Hawthorne inspires staff with his vision of “hospitality that feels like family”.\nAnalyse one way in which this transformational leadership might improve Hawthorne Hotels’ performance.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Transformational leadership: inspires employees with a vision and encourages commitment and innovation (AO1).","Edmund shares his vision and staff say he inspires them (AO2).","Inspired staff are more engaged and provide better service → higher guest satisfaction and repeat bookings → higher revenue and profit (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Hawthorne Hotels uses temporary and part-time staff in the high season.\nAnalyse one way in which using temporary staff might help the hotels match their supply to demand.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Matching supply to demand: ways of coping with seasonal demand include temporary and part-time employment (AO1).","Demand at coastal hotels is highly seasonal (AO2).","Temporary staff can be hired for peak months → increases capacity to serve guests when demand is high without the cost of permanent staff in quiet months → lower labour costs and better service (AO3). Could reduce quality/loyalty."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Hawthorne Hotels’ employee costs are under control.\nCalculate employee costs as a percentage of revenue for 2023 and 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Employee costs % of revenue = employee costs ÷ revenue × 100 (AO1). 2023: 4.86 ÷ 19.4 × 100 = 25.1%; 2024: 6.30 ÷ 21.0 × 100 = 30.0% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): employee costs grew by 30% while revenue grew by 8%, so costs rose from 25% to 30% of revenue - not under control.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): extra staff for new hotels may increase costs before revenue arrives; high turnover adds recruitment costs; hospitality wages are rising; need benchmarks; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Hawthorne Hotels should decentralise decision making.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Decentralisation: delegating decisions to managers lower in the hierarchy (AO1).","Managers have little authority; Edmund makes final decisions on everything, including menus (AO2).","Benefits: faster, local decisions, motivation and development of managers (Herzberg: responsibility); relieves Edmund so he can focus on growth; risks: inconsistency of service, loss of control, mistakes (AO3).","Judgement: likely to help as the company grows to eight hotels if standards are protected (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) decentralising decision making and (2) setting up a central training academy with standard procedures as ways of improving performance as Hawthorne grows, and recommend which one to choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Decentralisation: faster decisions, empowerment and retention of managers; local responsiveness; but inconsistent standards and weaker control, need for skilled managers (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Training academy: consistent culture and service, skills for 30 new staff, lower turnover through development; costs, less flexibility and may not address managers’ lack of authority (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: turnover 38%; employee costs 30% of revenue; two new hotels; strong family culture (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: culture, Herzberg/Maslow, delegation, employee costs ratio, leadership styles (AO3).","Judgement: academy supports culture and growth; decentralisation addresses managers’ authority; a phased combination could be recommended but a clear choice is needed (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Redstone Brewery’s demand is highly seasonal.\nAnalyse one way in which seasonal demand might affect Redstone’s unit costs.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Seasonal demand: demand that changes at predictable times of the year (AO1); unit cost = total cost ÷ output (AO1).","Sales fall by 40% in January and February but fixed costs of tanks and brewery continue (AO2).","Lower output in off-peak → fixed costs spread over fewer litres → higher unit costs in quiet months → lower profit margin (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Redstone Brewery uses part-time and temporary staff in peak months.\nAnalyse one way in which this might affect the quality of its beer.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Part-time and temporary employment: flexible but less experienced and less committed (AO1).","Redstone is a craft brewer whose reputation depends on quality (AO2).","Temporary staff need training and may make more errors → inconsistent brewing → defective batches, complaints and damage to the craft brand → lower sales (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Redstone Brewery is making good use of its brewing capacity.\nCalculate the capacity utilisation for the year to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Capacity utilisation = actual output ÷ maximum capacity × 100 (AO1) = 1,860,000 ÷ 2,400,000 × 100 = 77.5% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): about one-fifth of capacity is unused on average, so fixed costs are spread over fewer litres; but in summer the brewery is at full capacity and turned away orders.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): the annual average hides the seasonal pattern; the problem is seasonal capacity, not total capacity; judgement on whether utilisation is good."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Redstone Brewery’s unit costs suggest that it is operating profitably.\nCalculate the average (unit) cost and average revenue per litre to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Unit cost = total cost ÷ output = £5,580,000 ÷ 1,860,000 = £3.00 per litre; average revenue = £8,370,000 ÷ 1,860,000 = £4.50 (AO1, AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): profit per litre = £1.50 (a margin of 33%); total profit £2,790,000.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): profit would rise with higher capacity utilisation as fixed costs are spread; but unit costs on new tanks would be higher initially; price and costs may change; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) outsourcing production for the busy months and (2) investing £1.5m in four new fermentation tanks as ways of meeting demand and recommend which one Redstone should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Outsourcing: flexible, no capital investment, meets peak demand and keeps supermarket customers; loses control of quality, higher cost per litre, risk to craft brand, reliance on a partner (AO1, AO2, AO3).","New tanks: 25% more capacity (about 600,000 litres), economies of scale, control of quality; £1.5m cost, spare capacity in winter (utilisation already only 78%), payback depends on demand growth (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: demand growing steadily; supermarkets turned away; seasonal pattern; craft reputation (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: capacity utilisation, unit costs, scale (economies/diseconomies), investment appraisal, JIT/inventory management (AO3).","Judgement: weigh flexibility v control; the investment suits steady growth but increases fixed costs in the off-peak; recommendation must be justified (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Hawthorne Hotels Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Hawthorne Hotels Ltd owns six boutique hotels in coastal towns. The founder, Edmund Hawthorne, believes that guests come “for the people as much as the rooms”. He is a visible leader, visiting each hotel every month and sharing his vision of “hospitality that feels like family”. Staff say that he inspires them but that he makes final decisions on everything, even the choice of menu items."},{"t":"p","x":"The company has a strong culture of customer service, but some hotel managers complain that they have little authority. High season creates large changes in demand and the hotels use temporary and part-time staff. Hawthorne is planning to open two new hotels and needs to recruit and train 30 new staff. Employee turnover is 38% and absenteeism is rising."},{"t":"p","x":"The directors are considering two proposals: (1) decentralising decision making by giving hotel managers authority over budgets, menus and recruitment, or (2) a central training academy with standard procedures for all hotels."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Hawthorne Hotels Ltd employee data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Number of employees","180","210"],["Employee costs (£m)","4.86","6.30"],["Revenue (£m)","19.4","21.0"],["Employee turnover","29%","38%"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Redstone Brewery Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Redstone Brewery Ltd brews craft beer in a single brewery in the north of England. Demand is highly seasonal: sales peak in summer and in December, and fall by 40% in January and February. The brewery has a fixed number of fermentation tanks, so the maximum number of litres it can produce in a year is limited. It uses part-time and temporary staff in peak months, and rents additional warehouse space at busy times."},{"t":"p","x":"Over the past three years demand has risen steadily. During last summer Redstone had to turn away orders from two large supermarkets because it did not have enough capacity. The production manager, Callum Ross, says that “keeping tanks full all year is impossible with this kind of product”. He wants to improve resource utilisation and meet demand more reliably."},{"t":"p","x":"The directors are considering two options: (1) outsourcing the production of some of its lower-volume beers to another brewery for the busy months, or (2) investing £1.5m in four new fermentation tanks to increase capacity by 25%."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Redstone Brewery annual data (litres)"},{"t":"grid","head":["",""],"rows":[["Maximum capacity","2,400,000"],["Actual output","1,860,000"],["Total costs","£5,580,000"],["Total revenue","£8,370,000"]]}]},"caseNames":["Hawthorne Hotels Ltd","Redstone Brewery Ltd"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-2-C","title":"RA10 Paper 2 · Set C"},{"id":"ra10-26-p3-c","paper":3,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"C","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Terra Textiles’ annual report described its supply chain as “ethical”.\nAnalyse one way in which Terra’s response to the newspaper report might be seen as “greenwashing” or “bluewashing”.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Greenwashing/bluewashing: misleading claims of environmental/social responsibility for PR rather than genuine action (AO1).","The report claimed pride in an ethical supply chain while a supplier used child labour (AO2).","If Terra only issues statements without audits/changes, stakeholders see it as PR → loss of trust and boycott → lower sales and share price (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"The pound has fallen against the dollar.\nAnalyse one way in which this might affect Terra Textiles’ costs.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Depreciation of the pound: £ buys fewer dollars (AO1).","Terra buys cotton in US dollars (AO2).","Imports cost more in pounds → higher production costs → lower profit margins unless prices rise (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the 10% US tariff on imported clothing is likely to reduce Terra Textiles’ profit.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Tariff: a tax on imports; protectionism raises prices for foreign buyers (AO1).","Terra sells into the USA; a 10% tariff increases the price of its clothing in the US relative to American producers (AO2, AO3).","Lower demand if price elastic, or lower margin if Terra absorbs the tariff; Terra could respond by producing in the USA or finding other markets (AO3).","Judgement: depends on share of US sales and price elasticity (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess the likely effect of the supplier scandal on Terra Textiles’ stakeholders.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Stakeholders: shareholders, customers, employees, suppliers, community; stakeholder v shareholder approach; actions such as boycotts and activism (AO1).","Share price fell 12%; younger consumers threaten boycotts (AO2, AO3).","Shareholders lose value; customers may switch; workers in Bangladesh may lose work if Terra leaves; Terra’s reputation and employees’ morale (AO3).","Judgement on which stakeholders are most affected and how Terra should respond (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) moving production to Portugal and (2) automating the Bangladesh factory with an independent audit system as strategies for Terra Textiles and recommend which one it should choose.\nCalculate the net present value of option 2 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: NPV = Σ(cash flow × discount factor) − initial cost (AO1). PVs: 12 × 0.909 = 10.9; 20 × 0.826 = 16.5; 24 × 0.751 = 18.0; 24 × 0.683 = 16.4; 20 × 0.621 = 12.4; total 74.3; NPV = £4.3m (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): NPV is positive: the investment returns more than its cost in present value terms; payback is about 3.6 years (cumulative 12, 32, 56, 80 → £14m of year 4’s £24m needed).","Option 1: better labour standards, supports reputation and lowers tariff and transport costs (£9m saving) but £95m cost and £18m extra labour a year (net −£9m a year), 24-month delay, redundancies in Bangladesh (AO2, AO3).","Option 2: improves productivity and transparency, keeps low-cost production and jobs; audit may not remove all risk, tariffs and exchange rate risk remain, automation reduces jobs (AO2, AO3).","Judgement: weigh financial return, ethical risk and speed; sophisticated concepts: ESG, stakeholder approach, global strategy (off-shoring/re-shoring), investment appraisal; challenge the data (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Lakeside Telecom is planning to use AI to manage its network and handle customer calls.\nAnalyse one way in which AI might improve Lakeside’s competitiveness.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["AI: technology that can analyse data and automate tasks; digital technology in operations/marketing (AO1).","Lakeside’s network is old, customers complain about coverage and calls (AO2).","AI predicts faults and automates calls → faster response and lower costs → improved service and lower prices/higher margins → better competitiveness (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Trade unions have said that they will oppose the job cuts at Lakeside Telecom.\nAnalyse one way in which opposition from trade unions might affect the success of Lakeside’s strategy.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Resistance to change: reasons include self-interest and misunderstanding (Kotter and Schlesinger); trade unions as collective representation (AO1).","1 500 of 9 000 jobs might be cut; unions may strike (AO2).","Industrial action disrupts service → customer satisfaction falls further; delays implementation; morale falls → strategy fails or costs more (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Lakeside Telecom’s external environment is more of a threat than an opportunity.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["External environment: technological change (AI, disruptive technologies), competition (Porter’s five forces), economic and social factors (AO1).","Threats: new entrants, satellite service (substitutes), customer expectations; share fell from 18% to 14% (AO2, AO3).","Opportunities: AI and new network technology can lower costs and improve service; demand for data is growing (AO3).","Judgement: mostly a threat if Lakeside does not respond, but opportunities exist for firms that adapt (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Lewin’s force field analysis suggests that Lakeside Telecom can successfully introduce the new strategy.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Lewin’s force field: change succeeds when driving forces exceed restraining forces (AO1).","Driving forces: falling market share and satisfaction, new competitors, technology, shareholder pressure; restraining forces: union opposition, cautious hierarchical culture, fear of job losses, cost (AO2, AO3).","To succeed: strengthen drivers (communicate urgency), weaken restrainers (consult unions, retrain, protect service) (AO3).","Judgement: restraining forces are strong but manageable with leadership and communication (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) forming a joint venture with a global technology company and (2) building the new network and AI capability itself, and recommend which one Lakeside should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Joint venture: shares cost, risk and expertise, speeds up the transformation; loses some control and profit, culture clash, dependence on partner (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Organic (internal) growth: full control and profit, builds own capability; slower, expensive, high risk given Lakeside’s cautious culture and declining share and revenue (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: market share 18% → 14%; customer satisfaction 61%; union opposition; shareholders’ conditions (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: Lewin, Kotter and Schlesinger, strategic drift, Porter’s five forces, risk management, stakeholder power/interest (AO3).","Judgement: weigh speed, risk and control; the joint venture is likely faster but must protect customer service and stakeholders; recommendation with justification (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Terra Textiles plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Terra Textiles plc makes sportswear and workwear and sells to retailers in the UK, Europe and the USA. In 2024 an investigation by a newspaper found that one of its suppliers in Bangladesh used child labour. Terra’s annual report had stated that it was “proud of its ethical supply chain” and the company is a member of an industry ethical-trade initiative. The company’s share price fell 12% on the day of the report."},{"t":"p","x":"Terra buys cotton in US dollars and sells mostly in sterling and euros. The pound has fallen against the dollar over the last year. A new government has promised tariffs of 10% on imported clothing, which will affect Terra’s sales into the USA. Customers, particularly younger consumers, say they will boycott brands associated with unethical labour."},{"t":"p","x":"The board is considering two strategic options: (1) moving production from Bangladesh to Portugal, which has higher labour costs but better labour standards, or (2) keeping production in Bangladesh but investing £70m in factory automation and an independent audit system."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Forecast extra net cash flows from option 2 (£m)"},{"t":"grid","head":["Initial cost","Year 1","Year 2","Year 3","Year 4","Year 5"],"rows":[["70","12","20","24","24","20"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Discount factors at 10%: Year 1 0.909; Year 2 0.826; Year 3 0.751; Year 4 0.683; Year 5 0.621."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B: Option 1 — moving to Portugal (fictional estimates)"},{"t":"grid","head":["","Value"],"rows":[["One-off cost of moving","£95m"],["Extra annual labour cost","£18m"],["Annual saving in transport and tariffs","£9m"],["Time to complete","24 months"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Lakeside Telecom plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Lakeside Telecom plc provides mobile and broadband services to 4 million UK customers. Its mobile network is old and customers complain about coverage. Smaller competitors using the latest technology have entered the market and a large technology company has launched a satellite-based service in rural areas. Lakeside’s market share has fallen from 18% to 14%."},{"t":"p","x":"The chief executive, Imran Sheikh, wants to transform the company by adopting AI to manage the network and handle customer calls, which would reduce the workforce by 1 500 of 9 000 employees. Trade unions have said that they will oppose the cuts and may strike. A group of institutional shareholders has said that it will support the plan only if there is a clear plan to protect customer service. The company’s culture is described as cautious and hierarchical."},{"t":"p","x":"The board is considering (1) forming a joint venture with a global technology company to build a new network and provide AI services, or (2) building the new network and AI capability itself through organic growth."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Lakeside Telecom plc data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2022","2024"],"rows":[["Market share","18%","14%"],["Customer satisfaction","74%","61%"],["Employees","9 000","9 000"],["Annual revenue (£bn)","3.2","2.9"]]}]},"caseNames":["Terra Textiles plc","Lakeside Telecom plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-3-C","title":"RA10 Paper 3 · Set C"},{"id":"ra10-26-p1-d","paper":1,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"D","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Zenith Bikes’ survey used a convenience sample of customers in cycle shops on a Saturday.\nAnalyse one way in which this might affect the reliability of Zenith’s market research.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Sampling methods: random, stratified, quota; convenience samples are not random and may not represent the target market (AO1).","The sample was mainly men aged 30 to 50 in cycle shops on Saturdays, but Zenith’s target market is commuters of all ages (AO2).","Biased sample → results overstate demand among groups that were not surveyed → inaccurate sales forecasts → Zenith may over-produce or set the wrong price (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Zenith’s existing models are in the maturity stage of the product life cycle.\nAnalyse one way in which an extension strategy might help Zenith’s existing models.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Product life cycle: introduction, growth, maturity, decline; extension strategies include advertising, rebranding, lowering price, adapting the product (AO1).","Existing e-bike models face a growing market but strong competition (AO2).","Adapting the product (e.g. new colours or better battery) or lower prices may renew interest → sales stay up for longer → delays the decline and keeps cash flowing to fund the Z9 (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Zenith Bikes has performed better than the UK e-bike market.\nCalculate Zenith’s market share for 2023 and 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Market share = Zenith sales ÷ market sales × 100 (AO1). 2023: 9.0 ÷ 180 × 100 = 5.0%; 2024: 11.2 ÷ 214 × 100 = 5.2% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): sales grew 24.4% while the market grew 18.9%, so share rose from 5.0% to 5.2%.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): Zenith is outperforming the market, but its share is small, the figures cover two years, and competitors with bigger budgets may respond; the market growth quoted (12%) differs from the data (about 19%) - the source needs checking."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Zenith Bikes should use price skimming for the Z9.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Price skimming: high initial price to earn high margins from early adopters, then lower later (AO1).","The Z9 has distinctive features (longer battery, lighter); survey shows 64% interest at £2 400 though the sample is biased; average e-bike price is £1 650 (AO2). High margins help recover £3.2m development cost (AO3).","Against: £2 400 is 45% above average price; sales volume may be low and rivals may copy features; PED unknown (AO3).","Judgement: viable if the product is truly differentiated and the target market is price-inelastic (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) price skimming at £2 400 and (2) penetration pricing at £1 800 as ways of launching the Z9 and recommend which one Zenith should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Skimming: high unit margin (about £600 more per bike), recovers development costs, premium image; lower volumes, slower share growth, risk of attracting rival imitations; depends on price inelastic demand (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Penetration: wins share quickly in a growing market (12%+ a year), builds brand and economies of scale; low margins may not recover £3.2m quickly and customers may see the Z9 as less premium; hard to raise prices later (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: product differentiation, maturity of existing models, survey bias, market growth (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: PED, product life cycle, Boston Matrix (Z9 as question mark/star), break-even (AO3).","Judgement: weigh profit v share; skimming is appropriate if the features are unique and demand inelastic; recommendation with justification; challenge survey data (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Suppliers have been asked to accept 90 days instead of 30 days to be paid.\nAnalyse one way in which this might affect the relationship between Maple & Main and its suppliers.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Payment terms and trade credit; ethics in finance (payment terms for suppliers) (AO1).","Longer terms from 30 to 90 days delay suppliers’ cash inflows (AO2).","Small suppliers may face cash flow problems → resent the change, raise prices, reduce priority or stop supplying → risk to Maple & Main’s supply and reputation (AO3). Benefit: improves Maple & Main’s cash flow."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Bryony Walsh will use zero-based budgeting next year.\nAnalyse one way in which zero-based budgeting might help Maple & Main to control its costs.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Zero-based budgeting: every item of expenditure must be justified from zero each period (AO1).","Costs of wheat, energy and shops rose in 2024 (AO2).","Managers must justify each cost → unnecessary or inefficient spending is identified and cut → lower costs and higher profit (AO3). Takes time and may cut useful spending."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Maple & Main’s ingredient cost performance in 2024 was a problem.\nCalculate the variance and say whether it is adverse or favourable.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Variance = actual − budget = £2,140,000 − £1,900,000 = £240,000 (AO1, AO2); 12.6% of budget; adverse variance because costs were higher than budgeted (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): an adverse variance of this size reduces profit by the same amount unless offset; ingredient prices rose.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): external causes (wheat prices) are outside management control; the budget may have been unrealistic; need other variances; judgement on whether it is a serious problem."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Maple & Main will have enough cash over the three months of the forecast.\nComplete the closing balances in Appendix A to support your answer.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: closing balances (£000): January 60 + 88 − 102 = 46; February 46 + 99 − 96 = 49; March 49 + 90 − 94 = 45 (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): the lowest balance is £45k in January/March with a small buffer; cash is positive but outflows exceed inflows in two of three months.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): forecast is an estimate; seasonal and ingredient prices; limited buffer; need receivables, payables and other data; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) debt factoring and (2) extending supplier payment terms to 90 days as ways of improving Maple & Main’s cash flow and recommend which one it should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Debt factoring: immediate cash (85% of invoices), reduces risk of bad debts; fee reduces profit, customers may see the business as financially weak; relates only to business customers (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Supplier terms: free (no interest) and improves payables days; damages supplier relationships, risk of higher prices or supply disruption, ethical concerns (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: stakeholders (suppliers, employees) are already concerned; margin pressure; small cash buffer (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: working capital, receivables/payables days, ethics in finance, stakeholder power and interest (AO3).","Judgement: weigh cost, speed and relationships; recommendation e.g. factoring because cost is known and avoids damaging suppliers, or extended terms if relationships are strong; justified (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Zenith Bikes Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Zenith Bikes Ltd designs electric bicycles for city commuters and sells them to cycle shops. It plans to launch a new model, the Z9, which has a battery that lasts twice as long as rivals’ and is 30% lighter. Zenith’s sales director, Ola Brandt, believes the Z9 could become a market leader. Zenith has spent £3.2m developing the Z9 over four years."},{"t":"p","x":"Zenith commissioned a survey of 300 cycle-shop customers in two cities. The survey used a convenience sample (customers who were in the shop on a Saturday). It found that 64% would “definitely” or “probably” buy a Z9 at £2 400, but the sample was mainly men aged 30 to 50. The UK e-bike market is growing at 12% a year, and the average e-bike sells for £1 650. Zenith’s existing models are in the maturity stage of the product life cycle."},{"t":"p","x":"Zenith’s directors are considering two pricing strategies for the Z9: (1) price skimming at £2 400, aiming for early adopters and high margins, or (2) penetration pricing at £1 800 to win market share quickly."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Zenith Bikes Ltd and the UK e-bike market (£m)"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Zenith sales","9.0","11.2"],["UK e-bike market sales","180","214"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Maple & Main Bakery plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Maple & Main Bakery plc is a UK bakery chain with 120 shops which sells bread, pastries and sandwiches. Its shares are listed on the stock exchange. In 2024 the company missed its profit target because wheat and energy prices increased. The finance director, Bryony Walsh, has said that she will use zero-based budgeting from next year to cut costs."},{"t":"p","x":"Maple & Main’s customers include commuters, local residents and business customers. A number of stakeholders are concerned about the company’s plans: staff worry about the shop closures that might follow, suppliers have been asked to accept longer payment terms (90 days instead of 30), and the local councils where it operates want the company to continue supporting community events."},{"t":"p","x":"The company is short of cash and is considering two ways to improve its cash flow: (1) using a debt factoring company to receive 85% of the value of invoices to its business customers immediately, or (2) asking suppliers to accept 90 days’ credit terms."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Ingredient costs budget and cash flow forecast"},{"t":"grid","head":["","Budget","Actual"],"rows":[["Ingredient costs, 2024 (£)","1,900,000","2,140,000"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Cash flow forecast (£000): opening balance £60 in January; inflows £88, £99, £90 for January–March; outflows £102, £96, £94 for January–March."}]},"caseNames":["Zenith Bikes Ltd","Maple & Main Bakery plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-1-D","title":"RA10 Paper 1 · Set D"},{"id":"ra10-26-p2-d","paper":2,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"D","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Some project teams at Brightside Education have unclear goals.\nAnalyse one way in which Hackman’s model of team effectiveness might explain why these teams are less effective.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Hackman: effective teams need a compelling direction, enabling structure, supportive context and expert coaching (AO1).","Teams have unclear goals and change membership frequently (AO2).","Without a compelling direction and stable structure, members do not know what to focus on → duplicated or missed work → delays and poorer quality → projects delivered late (27%) (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Brightside’s sales staff are paid commission while developers are paid fixed salaries.\nAnalyse one way in which this difference in reward methods might affect teamwork.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Commission: pay linked to sales; salary: fixed pay; financial rewards (AO1).","Sales and developers work in the same project teams (AO2).","Sales staff may over-promise to win contracts; developers feel that pay is unfair → conflict, lower motivation and cooperation → poorer teamwork and delayed projects (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Brightside Education’s workforce has become more productive.\nCalculate revenue per employee (labour productivity) for 2023 and 2024 to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Labour productivity (sales per employee) = revenue ÷ number of employees (AO1). 2023: £24,000,000 ÷ 120 = £200,000; 2024: £25,900,000 ÷ 135 = £191,852 (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): productivity fell from £200,000 to £191,852 (-4%) - revenue grew 7.9% and staff grew 12.5%.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): the measure ignores quality: late projects rose to 27% and satisfaction fell to 58%; new staff take time to become productive; hybrid working may affect results; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether remote and hybrid working is likely to be beneficial for Brightside Education.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Remote, hybrid and flexible working as a non-financial reward (AO1).","Brightside’s staff work from home three days a week (AO2). Benefits: lower office costs, wider recruitment, flexibility and motivation (AO3).","Risks: weaker teamwork, communication and culture; difficulties for new staff and monitoring; employee satisfaction fell from 72% to 58% (AO3).","Judgement: depends on the nature of work and how well teams are managed (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) team-based rewards and clearer team goals and (2) leadership development and coaching for team leaders as ways of improving performance at Brightside, and recommend which one Hamid should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Team rewards and goals: aligns effort, reduces resentment between commission and salary groups, creates a compelling direction (Hackman); risk of free-riding, cost, difficulty measuring team output (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Leadership development and coaching: improves coaching and structure; transformational leadership could inspire; builds retention; time and cost, results slow, may not fix unfair pay (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: late projects 27%; satisfaction 58%; remote working; fast growth (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: Hackman, Herzberg, Maslow, transformational leadership, employee costs, talent development (AO3).","Judgement: weigh immediate impact on goals and motivation; recommendation justified, possibly sequencing both (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Ironclad Engineering uses Just in Time for specialised alloys.\nAnalyse one way in which the fire at the supplier’s factory shows a disadvantage of Just in Time.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Just in Time: holds minimal inventory and orders parts as needed; risk of stock-outs (AO1).","The six-week shortage forced Ironclad to stop one production line (AO2).","No inventory buffer → production stopped → lost output and late deliveries to aircraft-engine customers → lost revenue and risk of penalties and damaged reputation (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Ironclad’s patents protect its precision-casting process.\nAnalyse one way in which patents might help Ironclad’s competitiveness.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Patent: legal protection of an invention for a period, preventing copying (AO1).","Ironclad invests heavily in R&D (AO2).","Rivals cannot copy the process without permission → Ironclad keeps differentiation and can charge higher prices or earn licence income → higher profit and return on R&D (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Ironclad Engineering is making good use of its production capacity.\nCalculate capacity utilisation to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: Capacity utilisation = actual output ÷ maximum capacity × 100 (AO1) = 5,400 ÷ 6,500 × 100 = 83.1% (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): about 17% of capacity is unused, so fixed costs are spread over fewer parts; but the shutdown after the fire may have reduced output.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): spare capacity allows flexibility for orders; the six-week stoppage may explain the shortfall; benchmarks and trend data needed; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the new foundry could be completed within 14 months.\nDetermine the critical path and project duration from Appendix A.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Paths: A (3) → C (8) → E (3) = 14; B (4) → C (8) → E (3) = 15; B (4) → D (2) → E (3) = 9; B (4) → F (5) = 9. Earliest start of C = max(A 3, B 4) = 4, so C ends month 12; E starts month 12 and ends month 15. Critical path B, C, E = 15 months (AO1, AO2). Float: A has 1 month; D has 6 months; F has 6 months (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): the project takes 15 months, not 14; any delay to B, C or E delays the project.","Evaluation (AO3, AO4): the estimates are uncertain; planning permission could be delayed; Ironclad can speed C by adding resources; the foundry would not be ready before the next shortage; judgement."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) building the new foundry in-house and (2) developing a second overseas supplier and holding larger inventories as ways of improving security of supply, and recommend which one Ironclad should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["In-house foundry: control of quality and supply, protects IP, potential scrap recycling and cost savings; £12m cost, 15 months to build, fixed costs and diseconomies, risk of under-use (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Second supplier plus inventory: faster, lower investment, reduces dependence on one supplier (supply chain diversification); higher inventory costs, transport and ethical/quality audit issues, less control (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: fire caused a six-week stoppage; capacity utilisation 83%; Just in Time; engineers’ innovation; customers require reliability (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: JIT v JIC, make v buy, supply chain management, network analysis, investment appraisal (AO3).","Judgement: weigh speed, cost and control; the second supplier is quicker and cheaper but the foundry gives long-term security; recommendation must be justified (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Brightside Education Ltd"},{"t":"p","x":"Brightside Education Ltd sells online tutoring and learning software to schools. It employs software developers, sales staff and tutors. Most employees work from home for three days a week. The company has grown quickly and the founder, Hamid Rahimi, has recruited 15 new staff in the past year."},{"t":"p","x":"Project teams at Brightside include developers, designers and sales managers. A recent review found that some teams work well together but others have unclear goals, change membership frequently and have little access to the resources they need. Employees say that “nobody tells us how our work fits the company’s strategy”. Sales staff are paid commission on contracts won while developers and tutors are paid fixed salaries; this has caused resentment in some teams."},{"t":"p","x":"Hamid is considering two ways of improving team effectiveness and performance: (1) introducing team-based rewards and clearer team goals, or (2) investing in leadership development and coaching for team leaders."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Brightside Education Ltd data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Number of employees","120","135"],["Revenue (£m)","24.0","25.9"],["Employee satisfaction","72%","58%"],["Projects delivered late","12%","27%"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Ironclad Engineering plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Ironclad Engineering plc makes precision parts for aircraft engines. It makes most components in its own factories but buys some parts from outside suppliers. A single supplier in Eastern Europe provides a specialised alloy; a fire at the supplier’s factory in 2024 caused a six-week shortage, forcing Ironclad to stop one production line. Ironclad operates a Just in Time system for alloys to keep inventory costs low."},{"t":"p","x":"The company invests heavily in research and development (R&D), and its patents protect its precision-casting process. A group of its engineers has said that they want to set up their own firm, and the company has started a programme encouraging staff to develop new ideas within the company (intrapreneurship). An environmental audit of Ironclad’s factories found that 22% of the raw material is wasted as scrap, which is not currently recycled."},{"t":"p","x":"The operations director, Sofia Marek, is considering two options: (1) bringing production of the specialised alloy in-house by building a new foundry (£12m), or (2) developing a second supplier in Asia and holding larger inventories of the alloy (Just in Case)."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Project to build the new foundry: activities (months)"},{"t":"grid","head":["Activity","Duration","Must follow"],"rows":[["A  Planning permission","3","–"],["B  Design foundry","4","–"],["C  Build foundry","8","A, B"],["D  Buy equipment","2","B"],["E  Install equipment","3","C, D"],["F  Recruit and train staff","5","B"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Factory output: 5,400 parts per month; maximum capacity 6,500 parts per month."}]},"caseNames":["Brightside Education Ltd","Ironclad Engineering plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-2-D","title":"RA10 Paper 2 · Set D"},{"id":"ra10-26-p3-d","paper":3,"source":"ra10","series":"RA10 practice (7138 format)","set":"D","year":"2028","items":[{"q":"01.1","main":1,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"The government withdrew grants for solar panels in 2024.\nAnalyse one way in which this change in government policy might affect Solis Solar’s sales.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Government policy: grants/subsidies lower the cost for customers; their removal raises the price (AO1).","Sales fell by 20% after grants were withdrawn (AO2).","Higher net price for customers → demand falls (price elastic) → lower sales and revenue → Solis may have excess installers and lower profit (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.2","main":1,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Interest rates have risen in the UK.\nAnalyse one way in which higher interest rates might affect Solis Solar’s costs and demand.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Interest rates: cost of borrowing and reward for saving (AO1).","Customers use loans to pay for solar installation; Solis may have borrowed to fund growth (AO2).","Higher rates → loans dearer → fewer customers can afford panels → lower demand; Solis’s own borrowing costs rise → lower profit (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.3","main":1,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the government’s clean-electricity target is more of an opportunity than a threat to Solis Solar.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Political and legal environment: government targets and regulation can create demand (AO1).","Requirement for new homes to have solar panels would increase demand; battery storage is a growing market (AO2, AO3).","Threat: uncertainty about government policy, cheap imports, competitors benefiting equally (AO3).","Judgement: likely to be an opportunity if Solis can compete on price and quality, but depends on government consistency (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.4","main":1,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess the effect of low-priced imports of solar panels from Asia on Solis Solar.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Competitive environment: rivalry and substitute/entry threats; import prices (AO1).","Solis competes with low-cost Asian panels; customers compare prices (AO2).","Price pressure → lower margins or lost sales → Solis may need to cut costs, differentiate on installation quality or sell different products (AO3).","Judgement: serious threat to margins but installation service and local brand offer protection (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"A"},{"q":"01.5","main":1,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) expanding into Ireland and the Netherlands and (2) launching a battery storage installation service as strategies for Solis Solar and recommend which one it should choose.\nCalculate the net present value of the battery storage option to support your answer.","quant":true,"ms":{"body":["Quantitative skills: NPV = Σ(cash flow × discount factor) − initial cost (AO1). PVs: 8 × 0.909 = 7.3; 11 × 0.826 = 9.1; 13 × 0.751 = 9.8; 13 × 0.683 = 8.9; 11 × 0.621 = 6.8; total 41.8; NPV = £5.8m (AO2). Own Figure Rule applies.","Interpretation (AO3): NPV is positive; payback = 3 years + (36 − 32)/13 = 3.3 years; ARR = ((56 − 36) ÷ 5) ÷ 36 = 11.1%.","Market development: £20m cost, £5m annual profit from year 2 (payback 4 years from year 2 start = 5 years); spreads risk across markets, uses existing skills; regulations, exchange rate risk and local competition (AO2, AO3).","New product development: uses existing installers and customers, new demand from solar owners and government targets; needs licensing deal, technical skills and investment, technology risk (AO2, AO3).","Judgement: weigh return, risk and fit with strategy and the declining core market; recommendation must be justified; challenge fictional estimates (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"A"},{"q":"02.1","main":2,"sub":1,"marks":6,"text":"Vega Foods recalled a batch of snack bars containing undeclared nuts.\nAnalyse one way in which a product recall might affect Vega’s reputation.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Reputational risk and product safety (ethics in operations); recall costs (AO1).","The recall cost £6m and led to a Food Standards Agency investigation (AO2).","Customers lose trust → switch to rivals and retailers may delist products → lower sales and profit; negative publicity (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.2","main":2,"sub":2,"marks":6,"text":"Vega Foods has grown by acquiring other companies.\nAnalyse one challenge Vega has faced in integrating Nutri Bars Ltd.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["External growth: acquisitions can create synergy but also challenges such as culture clash, diseconomies of scale (AO1).","Nutri’s managers say the culture is “completely different” (AO2).","Culture clash → low morale, resistance, managers leave → duplication and poor communication → costs rise and expected synergies are not achieved (AO3)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2"},"kind":"analyse","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.3","main":2,"sub":3,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether Vega’s growth strategy has been successful.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Growth, economies of scale, synergy and diseconomies; acquisition v organic growth (AO1).","Revenue grew from £410m to £505m (23%), but operating profit fell from £58m to £36m, margin from 12.1% to 7.1%; recall and integration costs (AO2, AO3).","Evidence of diseconomies (control and coordination) and risk from rapid growth (AO3).","Judgement: growth has increased size but not profitability; not successful so far (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.4","main":2,"sub":4,"marks":9,"text":"Assess whether the sustainability claim in Vega’s report is likely to damage the company.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Greenwashing: misleading claims; ESG reporting and independent verification; activism (AO1).","The claim that 100% of palm oil is sustainable is not independently verified; activists have challenged it (AO2).","If proved false → loss of trust, boycotts, regulators, investor concern (AO3). If supported by audits → strengthens brand (AO3).","Judgement: risk is high unless Vega verifies claims (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2, AO4 = 3"},"kind":"assess","aoSplit":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2,"AO4":3},"section":"B"},{"q":"02.5","main":2,"sub":5,"marks":15,"text":"Evaluate (1) acquiring Oatly Fields for £140m and (2) a period of organic growth and consolidation as strategies for Vega Foods and recommend which one it should choose.","quant":false,"ms":{"body":["Acquisition: fast entry to plant-based dairy (growing market), synergies and economies of scale; £140m cost (about 4 times operating profit), integration problems from Nutri, culture clash, no crisis management, gearing/finance risk (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Consolidation: improves integration, quality control and sustainability reporting, lower risk, lower cost; slower growth, risk of missing market opportunities and competitors moving ahead (AO1, AO2, AO3).","Context: operating profit fell from £58m to £36m; recall; weak crisis plan; sustainability challenges (AO2). Sophisticated concepts: Ansoff, economies/diseconomies of scale, risk management, ESG, stakeholder approach, Lewin (AO3).","Judgement: weigh growth v risk; given weak integration and risk management, consolidation looks wise; but the market opportunity matters; recommendation with reasoning (AO4)."],"ao":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 4, AO4 = 5"},"kind":"evaluate","aoSplit":{"AO1":3,"AO2":3,"AO3":4,"AO4":5},"section":"B"}],"cases":{"1":[{"t":"h","x":"Source A: Solis Solar plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Solis Solar plc installs rooftop solar panels on homes and businesses in the UK. Its sales boomed when the government offered grants and when energy prices rose, but sales fell by 20% in 2024 when the grants were withdrawn and interest rates rose, making finance more expensive for customers. The company employs 1 200 installers, many of whom would be difficult to replace."},{"t":"p","x":"Solis faces strong competition from low-priced imports of panels from Asia. The government has announced an aim to produce all UK electricity from clean sources by 2035 and plans to introduce a requirement for new homes to have solar panels. The company’s chief executive, Beatrice Lang, sees a growing opportunity in battery storage and has been approached by an American battery company about a licensing deal."},{"t":"p","x":"The board is considering two strategic options: (1) market development: expanding into Ireland and the Netherlands with its current solar installation service, or (2) new product development: launching a battery storage installation service in the UK."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Forecast net cash flows from the battery storage option (£m)"},{"t":"grid","head":["Initial cost","Year 1","Year 2","Year 3","Year 4","Year 5"],"rows":[["36","8","11","13","13","11"]]},{"t":"cap","x":"Discount factors at 10%: Year 1 0.909; Year 2 0.826; Year 3 0.751; Year 4 0.683; Year 5 0.621."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix B: Expansion into Ireland and the Netherlands (fictional estimates)"},{"t":"grid","head":["","Value"],"rows":[["Investment","£20m"],["Forecast annual profit from year 2","£5m"],["Main risks","Different regulations, exchange rate risk, local competition"]]}],"2":[{"t":"h","x":"Source B: Vega Foods plc"},{"t":"p","x":"Vega Foods plc is a UK food producer making breakfast cereals, snacks and plant-based foods. It is a mid-sized business with 3 400 employees and has grown by acquiring smaller companies. Its latest acquisition, Nutri Bars Ltd, was bought for £85m in 2023, but integration has been difficult: Nutri’s managers say that the company’s culture is “completely different”."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2024 a batch of Vega’s snack bars was found to contain undeclared nuts and was recalled, which cost the company £6m and led to an investigation by the Food Standards Agency. The company had no crisis management plan. Critics said that it had grown too quickly. Vega’s sustainability report claims that 100% of its palm oil is “sustainable”, but an activist group has argued that this is not independently verified."},{"t":"p","x":"Vega’s board is considering two ways of moving forward: (1) a further acquisition, buying a large plant-based dairy company, Oatly Fields, for £140m, or (2) a period of organic growth and consolidation, improving integration, quality systems and sustainability reporting."},{"t":"h","x":"Appendix A: Vega Foods plc data"},{"t":"grid","head":["","2022","2023","2024"],"rows":[["Revenue (£m)","410","480","505"],["Operating profit (£m)","52","58","36"],["Number of acquisitions in the year","1","2","1"],["Product recalls","0","0","1"]]}]},"caseNames":["Solis Solar plc","Vega Foods plc"],"twoCase":true,"total":90,"minutes":120,"key":"2028-3-D","title":"RA10 Paper 3 · Set D"}];
